<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0">
  <head><title>Physics: From Newton to the Frontier</title></head>
  <body>
    <outline text="Physics: From Newton to the Frontier" _note="Open source: https://github.com/BrianY28/subjectmap">
      <outline text="Newtonian mechanics" _note="1687-1830s · Newton's Principia (1687) set F = ma and universal gravitation, and Euler, Lagrange and Hamilton recast mechanics analytically.">
        <outline text="Newton's laws" _note="1687 · Isaac Newton&#10;The Principia states three laws: inertia, force proportional to change of motion, and equal and opposite action-reaction, giving F = dp/dt (commonly F = ma). Together they define inertial frames and reduce mechanics to solving differential equations.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Newton, I. (1687). Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica [Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy]. Royal Society (Joseph Streater).&#10;- Newton, I. (1999). The Principia: Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy (I. B. Cohen &amp; A. Whitman, Trans.). University of California Press.&#10;- Goldstein, H., Poole, C. P., &amp; Safko, J. (2002). Classical Mechanics (3rd ed.). Addison Wesley.">
          <outline text="First law: inertia" _note="A body persists in uniform motion unless acted on by a net force, which implicitly defines the inertial frames where the laws hold.&#10;Explainer (after publication): https://briany28.github.io/subjectmap/physics/explainers/first-law/"/>
          <outline text="Second law F = dp/dt" _note="The rate of change of momentum equals the applied force, so trajectories follow from integrating the equations of motion."/>
          <outline text="Third law: action-reaction" _note="Forces between two bodies are equal and opposite, which underlies momentum conservation and the center-of-mass theorem."/>
          <outline text="Galilean relativity" _note="The laws are form-invariant under uniform relative motion, so no experiment within a closed inertial frame reveals absolute rest."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Universal gravitation" _note="1687 · Isaac Newton, Henry Cavendish&#10;Newton proposed that every mass attracts every other with F = G m₁m₂/r², deriving Kepler's elliptical orbits and the Moon's fall from one universal law. Cavendish's torsion-balance experiment (1798) measured the Earth's mean density, and G was derived from his data later.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Newton, I. (1687). Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica, Book III [Mathematical Principles of Natural Philosophy]. Royal Society (Joseph Streater).&#10;- Cavendish, H. (1798). Experiments to determine the density of the Earth. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 88, 469–526.&#10;- Boys, C. V. (1895). On the Newtonian constant of gravitation. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London A, 186, 1–72.">
          <outline text="Inverse-square law" _note="The 1/r² force yields closed conic-section orbits, uniting terrestrial fall and celestial motion under one rule."/>
          <outline text="Kepler's laws derived" _note="Equal areas and the harmonic relation T² ∝ a³ follow directly from the inverse-square force law."/>
          <outline text="Shell theorem" _note="A spherically symmetric mass attracts external points as if all its mass were at the center, and exerts no net force inside a hollow shell."/>
          <outline text="Cavendish torsion balance" _note="The 1798 experiment measured the tiny gravitational attraction between lead spheres and determined the Earth's mean density; G was derived from his data later."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Calculus of variations" _note="1744 · Johann Bernoulli, Leonhard Euler, Joseph-Louis Lagrange, Pierre Louis Maupertuis&#10;Euler's 1744 treatise turned extremum problems for curves into differential equations, later streamlined by Lagrange into the Euler-Lagrange equation d/dt(∂L/∂q̇) − ∂L/∂q = 0. The method seeks curves making an integral δ∫L dt stationary rather than optimizing a function.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Euler, L. (1744). Methodus inveniendi lineas curvas maximi minimive proprietate gaudentes, sive solutio problematis isoperimetrici latissimo sensu accepti [A method for finding curved lines enjoying a maximum or minimum property]. Bousquet (Lausanne &amp; Geneva).&#10;- Lagrange, J.-L. (1762). Essai d'une nouvelle méthode pour déterminer les maxima et les minima des formules intégrales indéfinies. Miscellanea Taurinensia, 2, 173–195.&#10;- Goldstine, H. H. (1980). A History of the Calculus of Variations from the 17th through the 19th Century. Springer-Verlag.">
          <outline text="Brachistochrone problem" _note="Johann Bernoulli's 1696 challenge—the fastest descent curve—is the cycloid and launched the variational method."/>
          <outline text="Euler-Lagrange equation" _note="Setting the first variation of an action integral to zero yields the stationarity condition that later becomes the equation of motion."/>
          <outline text="Geodesics" _note="Shortest or extremal paths on curved surfaces are found by the same variational machinery, linking mechanics to geometry."/>
          <outline text="Principle of least action" _note="Maupertuis (1744) and Euler (1744) proposed that motion makes an action stationary."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Rigid-body dynamics" _note="1743-1765 · Leonhard Euler, Jean le Rond d'Alembert&#10;Euler's equations I₁ω̇₁ + (I₃ − I₂)ω₂ω₃ = M₁ (and cyclic permutations) describe rotation of a rigid body about its principal axes. D'Alembert's principle (1743) supplied a general way to handle constraints in extended bodies.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Euler, L. (1765). Du mouvement de rotation des corps solides autour d'un axe variable [On the rotational motion of solid bodies about a variable axis]. Mémoires de l'Académie des Sciences de Berlin, 14, 154–193.&#10;- d'Alembert, J. le R. (1743). Traité de dynamique [Treatise on Dynamics]. David l'aîné (Paris).&#10;- Goldstein, H., Poole, C. P., &amp; Safko, J. (2002). Classical Mechanics (3rd ed.). Addison Wesley.">
          <outline text="Euler's rotation equations" _note="Torque equals the rate of change of angular momentum in the body frame, producing coupled equations for the angular velocities."/>
          <outline text="Moment of inertia tensor" _note="A symmetric tensor relating angular velocity to angular momentum, diagonalized by choosing principal axes."/>
          <outline text="D'Alembert's principle" _note="Inertial forces plus applied forces do no virtual work, converting constrained dynamics into statics-like balance equations."/>
          <outline text="Intermediate axis instability" _note="Rotation about the axis of intermediate moment of inertia is unstable, now familiar as the tennis-racket or Dzhanibekov effect."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Lagrangian mechanics" _note="1788 · Joseph-Louis Lagrange&#10;Lagrange's Mécanique analytique recast all of mechanics in generalized coordinates using L = T − V and the Euler-Lagrange equations, with no explicit forces needed. Constraints are built in, and conserved quantities follow from symmetries of the Lagrangian.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Lagrange, J.-L. (1788). Mécanique analytique [Analytical Mechanics]. Veuve Desaint (Paris).&#10;- Goldstein, H., Poole, C. P., &amp; Safko, J. (2002). Classical Mechanics (3rd ed.). Addison Wesley.&#10;- Landau, L. D., &amp; Lifshitz, E. M. (1976). Mechanics (3rd ed.). Pergamon Press.">
          <outline text="Generalized coordinates" _note="Any set of independent parameters describing the configuration replaces Cartesian coordinates and automatically respects holonomic constraints."/>
          <outline text="Action principle" _note="Physical paths extremize the action S = ∫L dt, from which the equations of motion follow as stationarity conditions."/>
          <outline text="Cyclic coordinates and conservation" _note="A coordinate absent from L has a conjugate momentum that is constant, an early form of symmetry-conservation reasoning."/>
          <outline text="Lagrange multipliers" _note="Constraints are enforced by adding multiplier terms to the equations, and the multipliers give the constraint forces."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Hamiltonian mechanics" _note="1833 · William Rowan Hamilton, Carl Gustav Jacob Jacobi, Siméon Denis Poisson&#10;Hamilton's 1833 reformulation replaces second-order equations with first-order ones, q̇ = ∂H/∂p and ṗ = −∂H/∂q, for H = Σp q̇ − L. The flow becomes a geometry on phase space, leading to canonical transformations and the Hamilton-Jacobi equation.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Hamilton, W. R. (1834). On a general method in dynamics. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 124, 247–308.&#10;- Hamilton, W. R. (1835). Second essay on a general method in dynamics. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 125, 95–144.&#10;- Jacobi, C. G. J. (1866). Vorlesungen über Dynamik [Lectures on Dynamics]. Georg Reimer (Berlin).">
          <outline text="Phase space" _note="Positions and momenta form a 2n-dimensional space whose points specify complete states and whose flow preserves volume (Liouville)."/>
          <outline text="Hamilton's equations" _note="The pair q̇ = ∂H/∂p, ṗ = −∂H/∂q gives symmetric first-order dynamics generated by the Hamiltonian function."/>
          <outline text="Poisson brackets" _note="Introduced by Poisson in 1809, {f, g} encodes time evolution via ḟ = {f, H} and identifies conserved quantities."/>
          <outline text="Hamilton-Jacobi equation" _note="A canonical transformation makes H depend only on new momenta, reducing the dynamics to a single partial differential equation for the action."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Electromagnetism and optics" _note="1785-1900 · Coulomb, Young, Faraday and Maxwell unified electricity, magnetism and light in Maxwell's equations (1865), notably ∇×B = μ₀J + μ₀ε₀∂E/∂t.">
        <outline text="Coulomb's law" _note="1785 · Charles-Augustin de Coulomb&#10;Coulomb used a torsion balance to show that the force between point charges is proportional to q₁q₂ and falls off as 1/r²: F = (1/4πε₀) q₁q₂/r².&#10;Sources:&#10;- Coulomb, C.-A. de (1785). Premier mémoire sur l'électricité et le magnétisme. Histoire de l'Académie Royale des Sciences, 569–577.&#10;- Griffiths, D. J. (2013). Introduction to Electrodynamics (4th ed.). Pearson.">
          <outline text="Torsion balance" _note="Coulomb's torsion balance measured tiny electrostatic forces by balancing them against a twisted fiber."/>
          <outline text="Electrostatic 1/r² law" _note="The electrostatic force between point charges decreases with the square of their separation, establishing the first quantitative law of electricity."/>
          <outline text="Superposition of charges" _note="The net force on a charge is the vector sum of the forces from individual charges."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Wave theory of light" _note="1801 · Thomas Young&#10;Young's interference experiments revived the wave theory by showing that light from two slits produces alternating bright and dark fringes with spacing Δy = λL/d.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Young, T. (1802). The Bakerian Lecture: On the theory of light and colours. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 92, 12–48.&#10;- Young, T. (1804). The Bakerian Lecture: Experiments and calculations relative to physical optics. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 94, 1–16.&#10;- Hecht, E. (2017). Optics (5th ed.). Pearson.">
          <outline text="Double-slit experiment" _note="Young observed interference between light passing through two nearby slits, a signature of wave superposition."/>
          <outline text="Interference fringes" _note="Bright and dark bands arise from constructive and destructive superposition of light waves."/>
          <outline text="Principle of superposition" _note="Overlapping light waves add amplitudes, so their relative phase determines the resulting intensity."/>
          <outline text="Wave–particle debate" _note="Young's results challenged Newton's corpuscular theory and reopened the question of light's nature."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Ampère's force law" _note="1820s · André-Marie Ampère, Hans Christian Ørsted&#10;Ampère explained Ørsted's 1820 observation of a magnetic needle deflected by current and formulated the force law between current-carrying wires, with F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πr) for parallel wires.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Ampère, A.-M. (1826). Mémoire sur la théorie mathématique des phénomènes électro-dynamiques, uniquement déduite de l'expérience. Mémoires de l'Académie Royale des Sciences de l'Institut de France, 6, 175–388.&#10;- Ørsted, H. C. (1820). Experimenta circa effectum conflictus electrici in acum magneticam. Typis Schultzianis.&#10;- Griffiths, D. J. (2013). Introduction to Electrodynamics (4th ed.). Pearson.">
          <outline text="Ørsted's compass experiment" _note="In 1820 Ørsted found that a current-carrying wire deflects a nearby magnetic compass needle."/>
          <outline text="Ampère's circuital law" _note="The line integral of magnetic field around a closed loop equals μ₀ times the enclosed current."/>
          <outline text="Parallel-current forces" _note="Parallel currents attract when in the same direction and repel when opposite."/>
          <outline text="Current-element force law" _note="Ampère's integro-differential law gives the force between infinitesimal current elements, later superseded by field theory."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Faraday induction" _note="1831 · Michael Faraday&#10;Faraday found that a changing magnetic flux through a circuit induces an electromotive force, ε = −dΦ_B/dt, linking magnetism and electricity.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Faraday, M. (1832). Experimental researches in electricity. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 122, 125–162.&#10;- Faraday, M. (1839). Experimental Researches in Electricity (Vol. 1). Richard Taylor.">
          <outline text="Electromagnetic induction" _note="Relative motion of a magnet and coil or a changing current generates a transient current in the coil."/>
          <outline text="Magnetic flux" _note="Flux Φ_B is the surface integral of B, and its rate of change determines the induced EMF."/>
          <outline text="Lines of force" _note="Faraday introduced intuitive field lines to describe magnetic and electric action through space."/>
          <outline text="Faraday disk" _note="The homopolar generator produced a continuous current from a rotating conducting disk in a magnetic field."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Maxwell equations" _note="1865 · James Clerk Maxwell, Oliver Heaviside&#10;Maxwell's 1865 paper set out electromagnetism as roughly 20 component equations, adding displacement current so that ∇×B = μ₀J + μ₀ε₀∂E/∂t and predicting c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Maxwell, J. C. (1865). A dynamical theory of the electromagnetic field. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 155, 459–512.&#10;- Maxwell, J. C. (1873). A Treatise on Electricity and Magnetism. Clarendon Press.&#10;- Jackson, J. D. (1999). Classical Electrodynamics (3rd ed.). Wiley.">
          <outline text="Displacement current" _note="A changing electric field acts as a current source of magnetic field, making Ampère's law consistent with charge conservation."/>
          <outline text="Four field equations" _note="Maxwell's 1865 paper listed about 20 equations in component form; Heaviside's 1884 reformulation condensed them into the four vector equations relating E, B, charge and current."/>
          <outline text="Electromagnetic theory of light" _note="Maxwell identified light as a propagating oscillation of electric and magnetic fields."/>
          <outline text="Luminiferous aether" _note="Maxwell's waves were initially thought to propagate through a mechanical aether, later discarded."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Electromagnetic waves" _note="1888 · Heinrich Hertz&#10;Hertz generated and detected radio waves with spark-gap apparatus, confirming Maxwell's prediction that light is an electromagnetic wave travelling at c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Hertz, H. (1888). Über elektromagnetische Wellen im Luftraume und deren Reflexion [On electromagnetic waves in air and their reflection]. Annalen der Physik und Chemie, 270, 609–623.&#10;- Hertz, H. (1893). Electric Waves: Being Researches on the Propagation of Electric Action with Finite Velocity Through Space (D. E. Jones, Trans.). Macmillan.">
          <outline text="Spark-gap oscillator" _note="A high-voltage spark drove oscillating currents in a dipole-like antenna, radiating electromagnetic waves."/>
          <outline text="Resonant receiver loop" _note="A small loop with a spark gap detected the arriving waves by resonant induction."/>
          <outline text="Reflection and polarization" _note="Hertz showed that the waves could be reflected, refracted and polarized like light."/>
          <outline text="Speed-of-light measurement" _note="Hertz used standing waves to measure the wavelength and inferred a speed close to that of light."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Lorentz force law" _note="1892 · Hendrik Antoon Lorentz&#10;Lorentz formulated the force on a charge moving in electric and magnetic fields, F = q(E + v×B), completing the classical dynamics of charged particles.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Lorentz, H. A. (1892). La théorie électromagnétique de Maxwell et son application aux corps mouvants. Archives Néerlandaises des Sciences Exactes et Naturelles, 25, 363–552.&#10;- Lorentz, H. A. (1895). Versuch einer Theorie der electrischen und optischen Erscheinungen in bewegten Körpern [Attempt of a theory of electrical and optical phenomena in moving bodies]. Brill.&#10;- Jackson, J. D. (1999). Classical Electrodynamics (3rd ed.). Wiley.">
          <outline text="Force on moving charge" _note="The electric term qE accelerates a charge while the magnetic term qv×B acts perpendicular to velocity."/>
          <outline text="Charged-particle trajectories" _note="In a uniform magnetic field the Lorentz force produces circular or helical motion with cyclotron frequency qB/m."/>
          <outline text="Electron theory of matter" _note="Lorentz treated matter as composed of charged particles whose motion produced electromagnetic phenomena."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Thermodynamics and statistical mechanics" _note="1824-1902 · Carnot, Joule, Clausius and Boltzmann established the laws of thermodynamics and the statistical entropy S = k log W.">
        <outline text="Carnot cycle" _note="1824 · Sadi Carnot&#10;Carnot analyzed an idealized reversible heat engine within caloric theory and showed that its maximum efficiency depends only on the temperatures of the two reservoirs, not on the working substance. The modern formula η = 1 - T_C/T_H came later, after Kelvin's absolute temperature scale (1848) and Clausius's work in the 1850s.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Carnot, S. (1824). Réflexions sur la puissance motrice du feu et sur les machines propres à développer cette puissance. Paris: Bachelier.&#10;- Fermi, E. (1937). Thermodynamics. New York: Prentice-Hall.">
          <outline text="Carnot efficiency" _note="Carnot showed the maximum efficiency depends only on the two reservoir temperatures; its modern form η = 1 - T_C/T_H follows from the absolute temperature scale and the second law."/>
          <outline text="Reversible cycle legs" _note="The cycle alternates isothermal expansion and compression with adiabatic expansion and compression."/>
          <outline text="Carnot theorem" _note="No engine operating between the same two reservoirs can exceed the efficiency of a reversible Carnot engine."/>
          <outline text="Reversibility and maximum work" _note="Irreversible processes reduce work output, motivating the later concept of entropy."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="First law of thermodynamics" _note="1840s · Julius von Mayer, James Prescott Joule, Hermann von Helmholtz&#10;Heat and mechanical work are equivalent forms of energy, and the internal energy of a system is a state function: ΔU = Q - W, with work done by the system. Joule's paddle-wheel experiments provided quantitative support.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Mayer, J. R. (1842). Bemerkungen über die Kräfte der unbelebten Natur. Annalen der Chemie und Pharmacie, 42, 233–240.&#10;- Joule, J. P. (1850). On the mechanical equivalent of heat. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 140, 61–82.&#10;- Helmholtz, H. (1847). Über die Erhaltung der Kraft. Berlin: Druck und Verlag von G. Reimer.">
          <outline text="Joule's paddle-wheel experiment" _note="Mechanical work churned water and raised its temperature, establishing the mechanical equivalent of heat in the 1840s."/>
          <outline text="Mechanical equivalent of heat" _note="Joule estimated about 4.2 J per calorie, linking work and heat quantitatively."/>
          <outline text="Internal energy as a state function" _note="The change ΔU depends only on initial and final states, not on the path."/>
          <outline text="Energy conservation" _note="The first law expresses conservation of energy for thermodynamic systems, including heat and work."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Second law of thermodynamics" _note="1850 · Rudolf Clausius, William Thomson (Lord Kelvin)&#10;Clausius and Kelvin stated the impossibility of certain spontaneous heat-flow and engine processes. Clausius later introduced entropy in 1865, yielding ΔS ≥ ∫ δQ/T, with equality for reversible processes.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Clausius, R. (1850). Über die bewegende Kraft der Wärme und die Gesetze, welche sich daraus für die Wärmelehre selbst ableiten lassen. Annalen der Physik und Chemie.&#10;- Clausius, R. (1865). Über verschiedene für die Anwendung bequeme Formen der Hauptgleichungen der mechanischen Wärmetheorie. Annalen der Physik und Chemie, 125, 353–400.&#10;- Fermi, E. (1937). Thermodynamics. New York: Prentice-Hall.">
          <outline text="Clausius statement" _note="Heat cannot flow spontaneously from a colder to a hotter body without external work."/>
          <outline text="Kelvin-Planck statement" _note="No cyclic engine can convert heat from a single reservoir entirely into work."/>
          <outline text="Entropy" _note="Clausius introduced entropy as a state function whose change measures reversible heat transfer divided by temperature."/>
          <outline text="Clausius inequality" _note="For any cyclic process ∮ δQ/T ≤ 0, with equality only when reversible."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Kinetic theory of gases" _note="1860 · James Clerk Maxwell, Ludwig Boltzmann, Rudolf Clausius&#10;Maxwell derived the molecular speed distribution f(v) ∝ v² exp(-mv²/2kT) and, with Boltzmann, connected macroscopic gas properties to microscopic collisions. Clausius had earlier introduced the mean free path.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Maxwell, J. C. (1860). Illustrations of the dynamical theory of gases. Philosophical Magazine, 19, 19–32; 20, 21–37.&#10;- Boltzmann, L. (1872). Weitere Studien über das Wärmegleichgewicht unter Gasmolekülen. Sitzungsberichte der Kaiserlichen Akademie der Wissenschaften in Wien, 66, 275–370.&#10;- Chapman, S., &amp; Cowling, T. G. (1970). The Mathematical Theory of Non-uniform Gases (3rd ed.). Cambridge: Cambridge University Press.">
          <outline text="Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution" _note="Molecular speeds follow f(v) ∝ v² exp(-mv²/2kT) in thermal equilibrium."/>
          <outline text="Mean free path" _note="Clausius introduced the average distance a molecule travels between collisions."/>
          <outline text="Equipartition theorem" _note="Each quadratic degree of freedom contributes ½kT to the average energy in classical thermal equilibrium."/>
          <outline text="Transport coefficients" _note="Kinetic theory relates viscosity, thermal conductivity and diffusion to molecular collisions."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Boltzmann equation" _note="1872 · Ludwig Boltzmann&#10;Boltzmann's integro-differential equation describes how a one-particle distribution evolves under external forces and binary collisions: ∂f/∂t + v·∇_r f + (F/m)·∇_v f = (∂f/∂t)_coll.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Boltzmann, L. (1872). Weitere Studien über das Wärmegleichgewicht unter Gasmolekülen. Sitzungsberichte der Kaiserlichen Akademie der Wissenschaften in Wien, 66, 275–370.&#10;- Cercignani, C. (1988). The Boltzmann Equation and Its Applications. New York: Springer-Verlag.">
          <outline text="Collision integral" _note="The right-hand side sums changes in f from binary molecular collisions."/>
          <outline text="Molecular chaos (Stosszahlansatz)" _note="Boltzmann assumed pre-collision velocities are statistically uncorrelated, introducing irreversibility."/>
          <outline text="H-theorem" _note="Boltzmann showed his H functional decreases monotonically, connecting microscopic dynamics to entropy growth."/>
          <outline text="Reversibility and recurrence objections" _note="Loschmidt's reversibility and Zermelo's recurrence objections exposed tensions between microscopic reversibility and macroscopic irreversibility."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Statistical entropy" _note="1877 · Ludwig Boltzmann, Max Planck&#10;Boltzmann related entropy to the number W of microscopic configurations compatible with a macrostate, S = k log W. Planck later introduced the constant k and gave it Boltzmann's name.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Boltzmann, L. (1877). Über die Beziehung zwischen dem zweiten Hauptsatze der mechanischen Wärmetheorie und der Wahrscheinlichkeitsrechnung respektive den Sätzen über das Wärmegleichgewicht. Sitzungsberichte der Kaiserlichen Akademie der Wissenschaften in Wien, 76, 373–435.&#10;- Planck, M. (1901). Über das Gesetz der Energieverteilung im Normalspektrum. Annalen der Physik, 4, 553–563.&#10;- Pathria, R. K., &amp; Beale, P. D. (2011). Statistical Mechanics (3rd ed.). Amsterdam: Elsevier/Academic Press.">
          <outline text="Microstates and macrostates" _note="A macrostate's entropy counts the number W of microscopic configurations consistent with it."/>
          <outline text="Boltzmann entropy formula" _note="S = k log W links thermodynamic entropy to statistical multiplicity."/>
          <outline text="Boltzmann constant" _note="The constant k ≈ 1.38 × 10⁻²³ J/K was introduced by Planck in 1900."/>
          <outline text="Second-law interpretation" _note="Equilibrium corresponds to the overwhelmingly most probable macrostate, explaining entropy increase statistically."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Gibbs ensembles" _note="1902 · Josiah Willard Gibbs&#10;Gibbs formulated statistical mechanics through ensembles of microscopic states, with canonical probabilities P ∝ exp(-H/kT) and thermodynamic quantities derived from partition functions such as F = -kT ln Z.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Gibbs, J. W. (1902). Elementary Principles in Statistical Mechanics: Developed with Especial Reference to the Rational Foundation of Thermodynamics. New York: Charles Scribner's Sons.&#10;- Tolman, R. C. (1938). The Principles of Statistical Mechanics. Oxford: Oxford University Press.">
          <outline text="Microcanonical ensemble" _note="An isolated system with fixed energy is described by a uniform distribution over accessible microstates."/>
          <outline text="Canonical ensemble" _note="A system in contact with a heat bath has P ∝ exp(-H/kT), yielding the Helmholtz free energy."/>
          <outline text="Grand canonical ensemble" _note="A system exchanging energy and particles with a reservoir uses a grand partition function and chemical potential."/>
          <outline text="Partition function" _note="The normalization Z encodes the statistical sum from which thermodynamic observables follow."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Quantum mechanics and QFT" _note="1900-1970s · Planck's E = hν, Schrödinger's iħ∂ψ/∂t = Ĥψ and Dirac's equation grew into quantum field theory and gauge theory.">
        <outline text="Planck quantum" _note="1900 · Max Planck&#10;Planck explained blackbody radiation by assuming oscillator energies are emitted or absorbed in discrete quanta E = hν, introducing the Planck constant h.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Planck, M. (1901). Über das Gesetz der Energieverteilung im Normalspectrum [On the law of energy distribution in the normal spectrum]. Annalen der Physik, 309, 553–563.&#10;- Planck, M. (1906). Vorlesungen über die Theorie der Wärmestrahlung [Lectures on the theory of heat radiation]. Leipzig: Johann Ambrosius Barth.">
          <outline text="Blackbody radiation law" _note="Planck's law, fitted to Rubens and Kurlbaum's precise infrared measurements, interpolates between the Wien and Rayleigh–Jeans limits."/>
          <outline text="Ultraviolet catastrophe" _note="The name, coined by Ehrenfest in 1911, describes the classical Rayleigh–Jeans divergence at short wavelengths; Planck's 1900 derivation predates it."/>
          <outline text="Energy quantization" _note="Energy exchange occurs in units E = hν, where ν is frequency."/>
          <outline text="Planck constant" _note="h ≈ 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s sets the quantum of action."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Bohr atom" _note="1913 · Niels Bohr&#10;Bohr proposed quantized stationary electron orbits with angular momentum L = nħ, with radiation emitted only in transitions hν = E_i − E_f.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bohr, N. (1913). On the constitution of atoms and molecules. Philosophical Magazine, 26, 1–25.&#10;- Bohr, N. (1922). The theory of spectra and atomic constitution. Cambridge University Press.">
          <outline text="Stationary states" _note="Electrons occupy discrete orbits without radiating."/>
          <outline text="Hydrogen spectrum" _note="Transition energies reproduce the Balmer and Lyman series."/>
          <outline text="Bohr radius" _note="a₀ = 4πε₀ħ²/(m_e e²) ≈ 0.529 Å sets atomic size."/>
          <outline text="Correspondence principle" _note="Quantum predictions approach classical physics for large n."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Matrix and wave mechanics" _note="1925-1926 · Werner Heisenberg, Erwin Schrödinger, Max Born, Pascual Jordan&#10;Heisenberg's matrix mechanics (1925) and Schrödinger's wave mechanics (1926) gave two equivalent formulations of quantum mechanics. The wave equation is iħ∂ψ/∂t = Ĥψ.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Heisenberg, W. (1925). Über quantentheoretische Umdeutung kinematischer und mechanischer Beziehungen [On the quantum-theoretical reinterpretation of kinematical and mechanical relations]. Zeitschrift für Physik, 33, 879–893.&#10;- Schrödinger, E. (1926). Quantisierung als Eigenwertproblem [Quantization as an eigenvalue problem]. Annalen der Physik, 79, 361–376.&#10;- Born, M. (1926). Zur Quantenmechanik der Stoßvorgänge [On the quantum mechanics of collisions]. Zeitschrift für Physik, 37, 863–867.">
          <outline text="Heisenberg matrix mechanics" _note="Observables are noncommuting matrices; [x,p] = iħ."/>
          <outline text="Schrödinger equation" _note="The wavefunction evolves according to iħ∂ψ/∂t = Ĥψ."/>
          <outline text="Born probability rule" _note="|ψ|² gives the probability density for measurement outcomes."/>
          <outline text="Transformation theory" _note="Dirac and Jordan formalized the equivalence of matrix and wave mechanics."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Uncertainty principle" _note="1927 · Werner Heisenberg, Earle Kennard&#10;Heisenberg's 1927 paper gave an order-of-magnitude estimate; Earle Kennard proved the exact bound Δx Δp ≥ ħ/2 that same year, and Robertson (1929) generalized it to arbitrary noncommuting observables.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Heisenberg, W. (1927). Über den anschaulichen Inhalt der quantentheoretischen Kinematik und Mechanik [On the perceptual content of quantum theoretical kinematics and mechanics]. Zeitschrift für Physik, 43, 172–198.&#10;- Kennard, E. H. (1927). Zur Quantenmechanik einfacher Bewegungstypen [On the quantum mechanics of simple types of motion]. Zeitschrift für Physik, 44, 326–352.&#10;- Robertson, H. P. (1929). The uncertainty principle. Physical Review, 34, 163–164.">
          <outline text="Position-momentum uncertainty" _note="Δx Δp ≥ ħ/2, proved by Kennard in 1927, limits simultaneous knowledge of position and momentum."/>
          <outline text="Energy-time uncertainty" _note="ΔE Δt ≳ ħ/2 constrains lifetime and energy spread, though its interpretation is subtle."/>
          <outline text="Complementarity" _note="Bohr argued wave and particle descriptions are complementary, not simultaneously applicable."/>
          <outline text="Measurement disturbance" _note="Measurement inevitably disturbs conjugate variables in the Copenhagen view."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Dirac equation" _note="1928 · Paul Dirac&#10;Dirac's relativistic wave equation iħ∂ψ/∂t = (cα·p + βmc²)ψ predicted spin-1/2 electrons and antimatter.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Dirac, P. A. M. (1928). The quantum theory of the electron. Proceedings of the Royal Society of London. Series A, 117, 610–624.&#10;- Dirac, P. A. M. (1930). The principles of quantum mechanics. Oxford: Clarendon Press.">
          <outline text="Relativistic wave equation" _note="The first-order equation treats space and time on equal footing."/>
          <outline text="Spin-1/2" _note="The four-component spinor automatically includes electron spin."/>
          <outline text="Antimatter prediction" _note="Negative-energy solutions were reinterpreted as antiparticles; Carl Anderson discovered the positron in 1932."/>
          <outline text="Dirac sea" _note="Hole theory interpreted negative-energy states as antiparticles."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Entanglement and Bell's theorem" _note="1935-1964 · Albert Einstein, Boris Podolsky, Nathan Rosen, Erwin Schrödinger, John Bell, Alain Aspect&#10;Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen argued in 1935 that quantum mechanics is incomplete; Bell showed in 1964 that any local hidden-variable theory obeys inequalities violated by entangled states.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Einstein, A., Podolsky, B., &amp; Rosen, N. (1935). Can quantum-mechanical description of physical reality be considered complete? Physical Review, 47, 777–780.&#10;- Bell, J. S. (1964). On the Einstein Podolsky Rosen paradox. Physics, 1, 195–200.&#10;- Aspect, A., Grangier, P., &amp; Roger, G. (1982). Experimental realization of Einstein-Podolsky-Rosen-Bohm gedankenexperiment: A new violation of Bell's inequalities. Physical Review Letters, 49, 91–94.">
          <outline text="EPR paradox" _note="The 1935 argument used entangled positions and momenta to question completeness."/>
          <outline text="Quantum entanglement" _note="Schrödinger coined the term for nonseparable correlations between subsystems."/>
          <outline text="Bell inequality" _note="Local hidden-variable correlations must satisfy bounds that quantum entanglement violates."/>
          <outline text="Bell tests" _note="CHSH (1969) made the theorem testable; Clauser (1972) and Aspect (1982) observed violations, loophole-free tests followed in 2015, and Aspect, Clauser and Zeilinger shared the 2022 Nobel Prize."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="QED renormalization" _note="1940s · Richard Feynman, Julian Schwinger, Sin-Itiro Tomonaga, Freeman Dyson&#10;Feynman, Schwinger and Tomonaga developed covariant QED calculations, and Dyson showed how to renormalize infinities into measured mass and charge. The electron anomalous moment a_e = (g − 2)/2 ≈ α/(2π) became a precision test.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Schwinger, J. (1948). Quantum electrodynamics. I. A covariant formulation. Physical Review, 74, 1439–1461.&#10;- Feynman, R. P. (1949). Space-time approach to quantum electrodynamics. Physical Review, 76, 769–789.&#10;- Dyson, F. J. (1949). The radiation theories of Tomonaga, Schwinger, and Feynman. Physical Review, 75, 486–502.">
          <outline text="Lamb shift" _note="The 1947 measurement of hydrogen 2S–2P splitting stimulated QED."/>
          <outline text="Renormalization" _note="Divergent parameters are absorbed into physical mass and charge."/>
          <outline text="Feynman diagrams" _note="Graphical perturbation rules organize QED scattering amplitudes."/>
          <outline text="Anomalous magnetic moment" _note="QED predicts g ≈ 2.0023 for the electron, matching experiment."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Gauge field theory" _note="1954 · Chen-Ning Yang, Robert Mills&#10;Yang and Mills generalized local U(1) gauge invariance to non-Abelian SU(2), introducing self-interacting gauge bosons. The field strength is F^a_{μν} = ∂_μ A^a_ν − ∂_ν A^a_μ + g f^{abc} A^b_μ A^c_ν.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Yang, C. N., &amp; Mills, R. L. (1954). Conservation of isotopic spin and isotopic gauge invariance. Physical Review, 96, 191–195.&#10;- Peskin, M. E., &amp; Schroeder, D. V. (1995). An introduction to quantum field theory. Addison-Wesley.">
          <outline text="Yang-Mills theory" _note="Non-Abelian gauge fields have nonlinear self-interactions."/>
          <outline text="Local gauge invariance" _note="Demanding invariance under spacetime-dependent transformations generates gauge interactions."/>
          <outline text="Non-Abelian field strength" _note="The f^{abc} term makes gauge bosons carry gauge charge."/>
          <outline text="Gauge boson masses" _note="Unbroken gauge symmetry predicts massless bosons, motivating spontaneous symmetry breaking in the 1960s."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Relativity and gravitation" _note="1905-present · Einstein's 1905 special relativity and 1915 field equations Rμν − ½R gμν = 8πG Tμν/c⁴ replaced absolute space and time with spacetime curvature.">
        <outline text="Special relativity" _note="1905 · Albert Einstein, Hendrik Lorentz, Henri Poincaré&#10;Physics is the same in all inertial frames and the vacuum speed of light c is invariant, so simultaneity and lengths become frame-dependent. The Lorentz factor is γ = 1/√(1 − v²/c²).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Einstein, A. (1905). Zur Elektrodynamik bewegter Körper [On the electrodynamics of moving bodies]. Annalen der Physik, 322(10), 891–921.&#10;- Poincaré, H. (1906). Sur la dynamique de l'électron [On the dynamics of the electron]. Rendiconti del Circolo Matematico di Palermo, 21, 129–176.&#10;- Lorentz, H. A. (1904). Electromagnetic phenomena in a system moving with any velocity smaller than that of light. Proceedings of the Royal Netherlands Academy of Arts and Sciences, 6, 809–831.">
          <outline text="Lorentz transformations" _note="They mix space and time between inertial frames while leaving the speed of light invariant."/>
          <outline text="Relativity of simultaneity" _note="Events simultaneous in one inertial frame need not be simultaneous in another."/>
          <outline text="Constancy of light speed" _note="All inertial observers measure the same vacuum speed c, independent of source motion."/>
          <outline text="Relativistic velocity addition" _note="Velocities combine as (u + v)/(1 + uv/c²), so no inertial frame reaches c."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Mass-energy equivalence" _note="1905 · Albert Einstein&#10;Mass is a form of energy, with a body at rest having energy E₀ = mc². Mass defects in bound systems therefore appear as released energy.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Einstein, A. (1905). Ist die Trägheit eines Körpers von seinem Energieinhalt abhängig? [Does the inertia of a body depend upon its energy content?]. Annalen der Physik, 323(13), 639–641.&#10;- Okun, L. B. (1989). The concept of mass. Physics Today, 42(6), 31–36.">
          <outline text="Rest energy" _note="A particle at rest has energy E₀ = mc², even with zero momentum."/>
          <outline text="Relativistic momentum" _note="Momentum p = γmv preserves conservation laws at speeds near c."/>
          <outline text="Mass defect" _note="Binding energy lowers a system's mass by Δm = E_b/c²."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Minkowski spacetime" _note="1908 · Hermann Minkowski, Albert Einstein, Henri Poincaré&#10;Space and time are unified into a four-dimensional spacetime with invariant interval s² = c²t² − x² − y² − z². This geometric formulation made Lorentz covariance manifest.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Minkowski, H. (1909). Raum und Zeit [Space and time]. Physikalische Zeitschrift, 10, 104–111.&#10;- Misner, C. W., Thorne, K. S., &amp; Wheeler, J. A. (1973). Gravitation. W. H. Freeman.">
          <outline text="Four-vectors" _note="Position, momentum and current combine into Lorentz-covariant four-vectors."/>
          <outline text="Light cone" _note="The causal structure separates timelike, lightlike and spacelike intervals."/>
          <outline text="World line" _note="A particle's history is a curve through four-dimensional spacetime."/>
          <outline text="Spacetime diagram" _note="Events are plotted with time versus spatial axes to display causal relations."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="General relativity" _note="1915 · Albert Einstein, Marcel Grossmann, David Hilbert&#10;Gravity is the curvature of spacetime sourced by stress-energy, and free bodies follow geodesics. The field equations are Rμν − ½R gμν = 8πG Tμν/c⁴.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Einstein, A. (1915). Die Feldgleichungen der Gravitation. Sitzungsberichte der Preußischen Akademie der Wissenschaften zu Berlin, 844–847.&#10;- Einstein, A. (1916). Die Grundlage der allgemeinen Relativitätstheorie. Annalen der Physik, 354(7), 769–822.&#10;- Wald, R. M. (1984). General Relativity. University of Chicago Press.">
          <outline text="Equivalence principle" _note="Local free fall is indistinguishable from inertial motion, motivating curved spacetime."/>
          <outline text="Einstein field equations" _note="They relate spacetime curvature to energy-momentum via Rμν − ½R gμν = 8πG Tμν/c⁴."/>
          <outline text="Geodesic equation" _note="Free particles follow extremal proper-time paths, d²xμ/dτ² + Γμ_αβ dxα/dτ dxβ/dτ = 0."/>
          <outline text="Gravitational time dilation" _note="Clocks deeper in a gravitational potential run slower than clocks higher up."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Schwarzschild solution" _note="1916 · Karl Schwarzschild, Johannes Droste, David Finkelstein&#10;The first exact vacuum solution of Einstein's equations describes a static spherically symmetric mass and has a coordinate singularity at r_s = 2GM/c²; its one-way horizon character was only clarified by Finkelstein in 1958. Its line element is ds² = −(1 − r_s/r)c²dt² + (1 − r_s/r)⁻¹dr² + r²(dθ² + sin²θ dφ²).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Schwarzschild, K. (1916). Über das Gravitationsfeld eines Massenpunktes nach der Einsteinschen Theorie. Sitzungsberichte der Königlich Preußischen Akademie der Wissenschaften zu Berlin, 189–196.&#10;- Finkelstein, D. (1958). Past-future asymmetry of the gravitational field of a point particle. Physical Review, 110(4), 965–967.&#10;- Chandrasekhar, S. (1983). The Mathematical Theory of Black Holes. Oxford University Press.">
          <outline text="Schwarzschild radius" _note="The horizon radius is r_s = 2GM/c², proportional to the mass."/>
          <outline text="Event horizon" _note="Finkelstein (1958) first showed that r = 2GM/c² is a one-way null surface, not a mere coordinate singularity."/>
          <outline text="Birkhoff's theorem" _note="The spherically symmetric vacuum solution is unique and static, even during spherical collapse."/>
          <outline text="Black hole" _note="A mass compressed within its Schwarzschild radius forms a region cut off from outside observers."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Tests of general relativity" _note="1919– · Arthur Eddington, Frank Dyson, Albert Einstein, Irwin Shapiro&#10;Eddington and Dyson's 1919 eclipse observations confirmed Einstein's predicted light deflection, the first of many precision tests. The deflection is δ = 4GM/(c²b) for impact parameter b.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Dyson, F. W., Eddington, A. S., &amp; Davidson, C. (1920). A determination of the deflection of light by the Sun's gravitational field, from observations made at the total eclipse of May 29, 1919. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A, 220, 291–333.&#10;- Shapiro, I. I. (1964). Fourth test of general relativity. Physical Review Letters, 13(26), 789–791.&#10;- Will, C. M. (2014). The confrontation between general relativity and experiment. Living Reviews in Relativity, 17, 4.">
          <outline text="Light deflection" _note="Starlight grazing the Sun is deflected by δ = 4GM/(c²b), twice the Newtonian value."/>
          <outline text="Perihelion precession" _note="General relativity explained Mercury's anomalous perihelion advance of about 43 arcseconds per century."/>
          <outline text="Shapiro time delay" _note="Radar signals passing near a massive body arrive later because of spacetime curvature."/>
          <outline text="Gravity Probe B" _note="Orbiting gyroscopes measured geodetic and frame-dragging precession around Earth."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Gravitational waves" _note="2015 · Albert Einstein, Rainer Weiss, Kip Thorne, Barry Barish, LIGO Scientific Collaboration, Virgo Collaboration, NANOGrav Collaboration&#10;Accelerating masses radiate ripples in spacetime, predicted by Einstein in 1916 and first detected directly by LIGO on 14 September 2015. The strain h = ΔL/L measures fractional stretching of space.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Einstein, A. (1916). Näherungsweise Integration der Feldgleichungen der Gravitation. Sitzungsberichte der Preußischen Akademie der Wissenschaften zu Berlin, 688–696.&#10;- Abbott, B. P., et al. (2016). Observation of gravitational waves from a binary black hole merger. Physical Review Letters, 116(6), 061102.&#10;- Abbott, B. P., et al. (2017). GW170817: Observation of gravitational waves from a binary neutron star inspiral. Physical Review Letters, 119(16), 161101.">
          <outline text="GW150914 detection" _note="The first direct detection came from two black holes merging about 1.3 billion light-years away."/>
          <outline text="GW170817 neutron-star merger" _note="In 2017 LIGO/Virgo and about 70 observatories observed the same neutron-star merger in gravitational waves and light, a kilonova that launched multi-messenger astronomy."/>
          <outline text="LIGO interferometers" _note="Kilometre-scale Michelson interferometers detect differential arm-length changes below 10⁻¹⁸ m."/>
          <outline text="Nanohertz background" _note="Pulsar timing arrays including NANOGrav reported evidence in 2023 for a nanohertz gravitational-wave background."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Nuclear and particle physics" _note="1896-2012 · Radioactivity, the nucleus and cosmic rays led to quarks, electroweak unification, QCD and the Higgs boson.">
        <outline text="Radioactivity" _note="1896 · Henri Becquerel, Marie Curie, Pierre Curie, Ernest Rutherford&#10;Becquerel discovered penetrating radiation from uranium salts in 1896, and the Curies discovered polonium and radium in 1898. The decay law N = N₀e^(−λt) describes the random decay of unstable nuclei, with half-life t₁/₂ = ln 2/λ.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Becquerel, H. (1896). Sur les radiations émises par phosphorescence [On the radiations emitted by phosphorescence]. Comptes Rendus de l'Académie des Sciences, 122, 420–421.&#10;- Curie, P., &amp; Curie, M. (1898). Sur une substance nouvelle radio-active, contenue dans la pechblende [On a new radioactive substance contained in pitchblende]. Comptes Rendus de l'Académie des Sciences, 127, 175–178.&#10;- Rutherford, E., &amp; Soddy, F. (1903). Radioactive change. Philosophical Magazine, 5, 576–591.">
          <outline text="Becquerel's uranium rays" _note="In 1896 Becquerel found that uranium salts emit penetrating rays without external excitation."/>
          <outline text="Curie radium discovery" _note="Marie and Pierre Curie discovered polonium and radium in 1898, showing radioactivity is an atomic property."/>
          <outline text="Alpha, beta, gamma rays" _note="Rutherford classified alpha and beta rays by 1899, and Villard identified gamma rays in 1900."/>
          <outline text="Exponential decay law" _note="The decay rate proportional to N gives N = N₀e^(−λt) and a fixed half-life for each nuclide."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Rutherford nucleus" _note="1911 · Ernest Rutherford, Hans Geiger, Ernest Marsden&#10;Rutherford interpreted Geiger-Marsden alpha scattering as deflection by a tiny, massive, positively charged nucleus, with most alpha particles passing through and a few scattering at large angles. The Coulomb scattering probability scales approximately as 1/sin⁴(θ/2).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Rutherford, E. (1911). The scattering of α and β particles by matter and the structure of the atom. Philosophical Magazine, 21, 669–688.&#10;- Geiger, H., &amp; Marsden, E. (1909). On a diffuse reflection of the α-particles. Proceedings of the Royal Society A, 82, 495–500.">
          <outline text="Gold-foil scattering" _note="Geiger and Marsden observed large-angle alpha deflections from thin metal foils in 1909."/>
          <outline text="Rutherford scattering formula" _note="The Coulomb scattering cross-section explains the observed angular distribution and the failure of Thomson's model."/>
          <outline text="Tiny nuclear size" _note="The nuclear radius is about 10⁻¹⁵ m, some 10⁴–10⁵ times smaller than an atomic radius."/>
          <outline text="Planetary atom model" _note="Electrons orbit a compact nucleus, though classical stability problems motivated quantum atomic theory."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Neutron and neutrino" _note="1930-1956 · Wolfgang Pauli, James Chadwick, Enrico Fermi, Frederick Reines, Clyde Cowan&#10;Pauli postulated a neutral, nearly massless particle to preserve energy and momentum in beta decay, while Chadwick's neutron and Fermi's theory established the nuclear ingredients. Neutrinos were directly detected in 1956 through inverse beta decay, consistent with n → p + e⁻ + ν̄_e.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Chadwick, J. (1932). Possible existence of a neutron. Nature, 129, 312.&#10;- Fermi, E. (1934). Versuch einer Theorie der β-Strahlen. I [Attempt at a theory of beta rays. I]. Zeitschrift für Physik, 88, 161–177.&#10;- Cowan, C. L., Reines, F., Harrison, F. B., et al. (1956). Detection of the free neutrino: A confirmation. Science, 124, 103–104.">
          <outline text="Pauli neutrino hypothesis" _note="In 1930 Pauli proposed a hidden neutral particle to explain the continuous beta-decay energy spectrum."/>
          <outline text="Chadwick neutron" _note="In 1932 Chadwick identified the neutral nuclear constituent with mass close to the proton."/>
          <outline text="Fermi beta-decay theory" _note="In 1934 Fermi treated beta decay as a weak four-fermion interaction."/>
          <outline text="Cowan-Reines detection" _note="In 1956 Reines and Cowan observed reactor antineutrinos via inverse beta decay."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Fission and fusion" _note="1938-1939 · Otto Hahn, Fritz Strassmann, Lise Meitner, Otto Frisch, Hans Bethe&#10;Neutron bombardment of uranium produced barium and lighter elements, which Hahn and Strassmann reported as fission and Meitner and Frisch explained via the liquid-drop model and mass-energy conversion. Bethe's contemporaneous work identified fusion reactions as stellar energy sources, with E = Δm c² governing the energy release.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Hahn, O., &amp; Strassmann, F. (1939). Über den Nachweis und das Verhalten der bei der Bestrahlung des Urans mittels Neutronen entstehenden Erdalkalimetalle [On the detection and characteristics of the alkaline earth metals formed by irradiation of uranium with neutrons]. Naturwissenschaften, 27, 11–15.&#10;- Meitner, L., &amp; Frisch, O. R. (1939). Disintegration of uranium by neutrons: A new type of nuclear reaction. Nature, 143, 239–240.&#10;- Bethe, H. A. (1939). Energy production in stars. Physical Review, 55, 434–456.">
          <outline text="Hahn-Strassmann fission" _note="In 1938 they found barium among neutron-irradiated uranium products."/>
          <outline text="Meitner-Frisch explanation" _note="In 1939 they interpreted fission as liquid-drop splitting with large energy release."/>
          <outline text="Stellar fusion cycles" _note="Bethe's pp chain and CNO cycle in 1938-39 explained hydrogen fusion in stars."/>
          <outline text="Chain reaction" _note="Fission neutrons can sustain a self-amplifying nuclear chain reaction."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Quark model" _note="1964 · Murray Gell-Mann, George Zweig&#10;Gell-Mann and Zweig independently proposed that mesons and baryons are composites of fractionally charged up, down and strange quarks, explaining SU(3) multiplet patterns. The proton is uud, giving charge +2/3 + 2/3 − 1/3 = +1.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Gell-Mann, M. (1964). A schematic model of baryons and mesons. Physics Letters, 8, 214–215.&#10;- Gell-Mann, M., &amp; Ne'eman, Y. (1964). The Eightfold Way. W. A. Benjamin.&#10;- Zweig, G. (1964). An SU(3) model for strong interaction symmetry and its breaking. CERN Report TH.401.">
          <outline text="Eightfold Way" _note="Gell-Mann and Ne'eman classified hadrons into SU(3) multiplets in 1961."/>
          <outline text="Fractional quark charges" _note="Quarks carry charges +2/3 or −1/3 and initially came in three flavors."/>
          <outline text="Hadron composition" _note="Baryons are three-quark states and mesons are quark-antiquark pairs."/>
          <outline text="Deep inelastic evidence" _note="Late-1960s SLAC scattering revealed pointlike charged constituents inside protons."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Electroweak unification" _note="1967 · Sheldon Glashow, Abdus Salam, Steven Weinberg&#10;Glashow, Salam and Weinberg unified electromagnetism and the weak force in a spontaneously broken SU(2)L × U(1)Y gauge theory, predicting W and Z bosons and neutral currents. The mixing angle relates the couplings through e = g sin θ_W.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Glashow, S. L. (1961). Partial-symmetries of weak interactions. Nuclear Physics, 22, 579–588.&#10;- Weinberg, S. (1967). A model of leptons. Physical Review Letters, 19, 1264–1266.&#10;- Salam, A. (1968). Weak and electromagnetic interactions. In N. Svartholm (Ed.), Elementary Particle Theory: Relativistic Groups and Analyticity (Nobel Symposium No. 8) (pp. 367–377). Almqvist &amp; Wiksell.">
          <outline text="Glashow SU(2)×U(1)" _note="In 1961 Glashow proposed the electroweak gauge group SU(2) × U(1)."/>
          <outline text="Weinberg-Salam model" _note="In 1967 Weinberg and Salam incorporated spontaneous symmetry breaking into the electroweak theory."/>
          <outline text="Neutral currents" _note="Gargamelle observed weak neutral-current events in 1973, confirming the theory."/>
          <outline text="W and Z bosons" _note="CERN discovered the W and Z bosons in 1983 at masses predicted by the theory."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Quantum chromodynamics" _note="1973 · Harald Fritzsch, Murray Gell-Mann, Heinrich Leutwyler, David Gross, Frank Wilczek, David Politzer&#10;QCD is a non-Abelian SU(3) gauge theory in which quarks carry color and interact via eight gluons. The strong coupling α_s(Q²) falls as Q² rises, giving asymptotic freedom while confinement keeps isolated quarks unseen.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Fritzsch, H., Gell-Mann, M., &amp; Leutwyler, H. (1973). Advantages of the color octet gluon picture. Physics Letters B, 47, 365–368.&#10;- Gross, D. J., &amp; Wilczek, F. (1973). Ultraviolet behavior of non-Abelian gauge theories. Physical Review Letters, 30, 1343–1346.&#10;- Politzer, H. D. (1973). Reliable perturbative results for strong interactions? Physical Review Letters, 30, 1346–1349.">
          <outline text="Color charge" _note="Quarks carry one of three color charges, while gluons carry color-anticolor combinations."/>
          <outline text="Gluon self-interaction" _note="Non-Abelian gluon fields carry color and interact with one another."/>
          <outline text="Asymptotic freedom" _note="Gross, Wilczek and Politzer showed in 1973 that α_s decreases at high momentum transfer."/>
          <outline text="Confinement" _note="No isolated colored particles are observed; hadrons are color singlets."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Higgs boson" _note="2012 · Peter Higgs, François Englert, Robert Brout, ATLAS, CMS&#10;ATLAS and CMS discovered a scalar boson near 125 GeV/c² at the LHC, matching the Higgs boson predicted by the Brout-Englert-Higgs mechanism that gives masses to W and Z bosons and fermions. Its scalar potential is V(φ) = μ²φ†φ + λ(φ†φ)².&#10;Sources:&#10;- Englert, F., &amp; Brout, R. (1964). Broken symmetry and the mass of gauge vector mesons. Physical Review Letters, 13, 321–323.&#10;- Higgs, P. W. (1964). Broken symmetries and the masses of gauge bosons. Physical Review Letters, 13, 508–509.&#10;- ATLAS Collaboration (2012). Observation of a new particle in the search for the Standard Model Higgs boson with the ATLAS detector at the LHC. Physics Letters B, 716, 1–29.">
          <outline text="Brout-Englert-Higgs mechanism" _note="In 1964 several groups showed that spontaneous breaking of a gauge symmetry can generate particle masses."/>
          <outline text="ATLAS/CMS discovery" _note="In 2012 the two LHC experiments reported a new boson near 125 GeV/c²."/>
          <outline text="Higgs couplings" _note="Observed production and decay rates are consistent with Standard Model mass generation."/>
          <outline text="Open questions" _note="The hierarchy problem, vacuum stability and possible Higgs partners remain unresolved."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Atomic, molecular, optical physics" _note="1814-present · The Planck-Einstein relation E = hν underpins spectroscopy, lasers and atomic clocks, making atoms and light precision probes.">
        <outline text="Fraunhofer lines" _note="1814 · Joseph von Fraunhofer, Gustav Kirchhoff, Robert Bunsen&#10;Fraunhofer mapped the dark absorption lines of the solar spectrum and labeled the principal ones, turning spectroscopy into a precision probe of matter. Kirchhoff and Bunsen later explained in 1859 that each element produces a characteristic set of lines.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Fraunhofer, J. (1817). Bestimmung des Brechungs- und Farbenzerstreuungs-Vermögens verschiedener Glasarten, in Bezug auf die Vervollkommnung achromatischer Fernröhre [Determination of the refractive and dispersive power of different kinds of glass with reference to the perfecting of achromatic telescopes]. Annalen der Physik, 56, 264–313.&#10;- Kirchhoff, G., &amp; Bunsen, R. (1860). Chemische Analyse durch Spectralbeobachtungen [Chemical analysis by spectral observations]. Annalen der Physik und Chemie, 110, 161–189.">
          <outline text="Solar absorption spectrum" _note="Dark lines arise when cooler solar gases absorb specific wavelengths from the continuous photosphere."/>
          <outline text="Fraunhofer D lines" _note="The prominent D lines near 589 nm were later identified with sodium."/>
          <outline text="Diffraction grating" _note="Fraunhofer's gratings enabled accurate measurement of spectral line wavelengths."/>
          <outline text="Kirchhoff–Bunsen law" _note="Each chemical element has a unique emission and absorption spectrum, founding analytical spectroscopy."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Balmer series" _note="1885 · Johann Jakob Balmer, Johannes Rydberg, Niels Bohr&#10;Balmer found an empirical formula fitting the visible hydrogen lines, 1/λ = R(1/4 − 1/n²) for n = 3, 4, ..., with R the Rydberg constant. Bohr derived it in 1913 from quantized electron orbits.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Balmer, J. J. (1885). Notiz über die Spectrallinien des Wasserstoffs [Note on the spectral lines of hydrogen]. Annalen der Physik und Chemie, 25, 80–87.&#10;- Bohr, N. (1913). On the constitution of atoms and molecules. Philosophical Magazine, 26, 1–25.">
          <outline text="Balmer formula" _note="The formula predicts the wavelengths of the visible hydrogen series from a simple integer relation."/>
          <outline text="H-alpha line" _note="The n = 3 to n = 2 transition gives the red H-alpha line at 656.3 nm."/>
          <outline text="Rydberg formula" _note="Rydberg generalized the pattern to 1/λ = R(1/n₁² − 1/n₂²) for all hydrogen series."/>
          <outline text="Bohr explanation" _note="Bohr's quantized angular momentum L = nħ reproduced the Balmer formula and introduced atomic energy levels."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Zeeman effect" _note="1896 · Pieter Zeeman, Hendrik Lorentz, Alfred Landé&#10;Zeeman observed that magnetic fields split spectral lines, and Lorentz explained the normal triplet using classical electron oscillators. The anomalous Zeeman effect later required electron spin and the Landé g-factor.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Zeeman, P. (1897). On the influence of magnetism on the nature of the light emitted by a substance. Philosophical Magazine, 43, 226–239.&#10;- Landé, A. (1921). Über den anomalen Zeemaneffekt [On the anomalous Zeeman effect]. Zeitschrift für Physik, 5, 231–241.">
          <outline text="Normal Zeeman triplet" _note="The normal pattern has frequency shifts Δν = 0, ±eB/(4πmₑ), equivalent to energy shifts ΔE = μB B m_l."/>
          <outline text="Anomalous Zeeman effect" _note="Many lines split into patterns not explained classically, pointing to spin and angular-momentum coupling."/>
          <outline text="Landé g-factor" _note="The g-factor parametrizes the magnetic moment of a coupled atomic state."/>
          <outline text="Polarization selection rules" _note="The emitted components have polarizations fixed by the change in magnetic quantum number."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Stimulated emission" _note="1917 · Albert Einstein&#10;Einstein introduced spontaneous and stimulated emission through A and B coefficients, showing that an incident photon can trigger emission of an identical photon. This process is the basis of masers and lasers.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Einstein, A. (1917). Zur Quantentheorie der Strahlung [On the quantum theory of radiation]. Physikalische Zeitschrift, 18, 121–128.">
          <outline text="A and B coefficients" _note="A₂₁ gives spontaneous emission, while B₁₂ and B₂₁ give absorption and stimulated emission rates."/>
          <outline text="Stimulated photon amplification" _note="The emitted photon has the same frequency, phase, and direction as the stimulating photon."/>
          <outline text="Population inversion" _note="A nonequilibrium state with more atoms in the upper level is needed for net stimulated amplification."/>
          <outline text="Einstein relations" _note="Detailed balance gives A₂₁/B₂₁ = 8πhν³/c³ and B₁₂ = B₂₁ for nondegenerate levels."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Atomic clock" _note="1955 · Louis Essen, Jack Parry, Isidor Rabi, Norman Ramsey&#10;Essen and Parry built the first practical caesium-beam atomic clock at the National Physical Laboratory, locking an oscillator to the Cs-133 hyperfine transition. The 1967 SI second was defined as 9,192,631,770 cycles of that transition.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Essen, L., &amp; Parry, J. V. L. (1955). An atomic standard of frequency and time interval: a caesium resonator. Nature, 176, 280–282.&#10;- Ramsey, N. F. (1950). A molecular beam resonance method with separated oscillating fields. Physical Review, 78, 695–699.">
          <outline text="Caesium hyperfine transition" _note="The Cs-133 ground-state hyperfine splitting provides a stable, reproducible frequency standard."/>
          <outline text="Ramsey separated fields" _note="Two separated microwave interactions produce a narrow interference fringe used to lock the clock."/>
          <outline text="SI second definition" _note="The SI second is defined by ν = ΔE/h = 9,192,631,770 Hz for the Cs-133 hyperfine transition."/>
          <outline text="Optical clocks" _note="Optical lattice and trapped-ion clocks, enabled by the optical frequency combs of Hall and Hänsch (Nobel 2005), now reach fractional uncertainties near 10⁻¹⁸, and a redefinition of the SI second is being prepared."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Laser" _note="1960 · Theodore Maiman, Arthur Schawlow, Charles Townes, Gordon Gould&#10;Maiman demonstrated the first laser, a flashlamp-pumped ruby rod, in 1960, using stimulated emission in a population-inverted medium. Schawlow and Townes had proposed the optical maser/laser concept in 1958.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Maiman, T. H. (1960). Stimulated optical radiation in ruby. Nature, 187, 493–494.&#10;- Schawlow, A. L., &amp; Townes, C. H. (1958). Infrared and optical masers. Physical Review, 112, 1940–1949.">
          <outline text="Ruby laser" _note="Maiman's ruby laser emitted at 694.3 nm and established the laser as a practical device."/>
          <outline text="Population inversion" _note="Optical pumping creates more atoms in the upper laser level than the lower level."/>
          <outline text="Optical resonator" _note="Mirrors provide feedback and select longitudinal and transverse modes."/>
          <outline text="Ultrafast and attosecond lasers" _note="Chirped pulse amplification (Strickland and Mourou, 1985; Nobel 2018) delivered tabletop petawatt pulses, while high-harmonic generation produced the first attosecond pulses (Agostini, Krausz and L'Huillier; Nobel 2023)."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Bose-Einstein condensate" _note="1995 · Satyendra Nath Bose, Albert Einstein, Eric Cornell, Carl Wieman, Wolfgang Ketterle&#10;Predicted by Bose and Einstein in 1924–25, a BEC was first realized in dilute atomic gases by Cornell and Wieman in rubidium-87 at JILA in 1995, followed by Ketterle's sodium condensate at MIT. Below a critical temperature, bosons macroscopically occupy the same quantum state.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bose, S. N. (1924). Plancks Gesetz und Lichtquantenhypothese [Planck's law and the hypothesis of light quanta]. Zeitschrift für Physik, 26, 178–181.&#10;- Anderson, M. H., Ensher, J. R., Matthews, M. R., et al. (1995). Observation of Bose-Einstein condensation in a dilute atomic vapor. Science, 269, 198–201.&#10;- Davis, K. B., Mewes, M.-O., Andrews, M. R., et al. (1995). Bose-Einstein condensation in a gas of sodium atoms. Physical Review Letters, 75, 3969–3973.">
          <outline text="Rubidium-87 condensate" _note="The JILA experiment cooled Rb-87 atoms in a magnetic trap to nanokelvin temperatures."/>
          <outline text="Laser cooling and trapping" _note="Magneto-optical traps and evaporative cooling, developed by Chu, Cohen-Tannoudji and Phillips (Nobel 1997), provided the ultracold samples that made BEC possible."/>
          <outline text="Macroscopic wavefunction" _note="The condensate is described by a single complex order parameter with coherent phase."/>
          <outline text="Gross-Pitaevskii equation" _note="Mean-field theory gives iħ∂ψ/∂t = (−ħ²/2m ∇² + V + g|ψ|²)ψ."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Condensed matter physics" _note="1911-present · Electrons in solids, superconductivity and many-body theory produced semiconductors, quantum Hall states and topological materials.">
        <outline text="Superconductivity" _note="1911 · Heike Kamerlingh Onnes, Walther Meissner, Robert Ochsenfeld, Fritz London, Heinz London, Alexei Abrikosov&#10;Onnes discovered zero resistance in mercury below 4.2 K in 1911, and the Meissner–Ochsenfeld effect (1933) showed flux expulsion, captured by the London equation ∇²B = B/λ_L².&#10;Sources:&#10;- Kamerlingh Onnes, H. (1911). Further experiments with liquid helium. D. On the change of the electrical resistance of pure metals at very low temperatures, etc. V. The disappearance of the resistance of mercury. Communications from the Physical Laboratory of the University of Leiden, No. 122b.&#10;- Meissner, W., &amp; Ochsenfeld, R. (1933). Ein neuer Effekt bei Eintritt der Supraleitfähigkeit. Naturwissenschaften, 21, 787–788.&#10;- Tinkham, M. (1996). Introduction to Superconductivity (2nd ed.). McGraw-Hill.">
          <outline text="Zero resistance" _note="Below a critical temperature, a superconductor carries current without measurable resistive dissipation."/>
          <outline text="Meissner effect" _note="A superconductor expels magnetic flux from its interior, distinguishing it from a perfect conductor."/>
          <outline text="London equations" _note="Phenomenological electrodynamics relates supercurrent to the vector potential and predicts a penetration depth λ_L."/>
          <outline text="Type-II vortices" _note="Abrikosov (1957) predicted quantized magnetic flux vortices in type-II superconductors above the lower critical field."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Band theory and Bloch theorem" _note="1928 · Felix Bloch, Alan H. Wilson, Rudolf Peierls&#10;Bloch's theorem (1928) shows that electrons in a periodic potential have wavefunctions ψ_k(r) = e^{ik·r} u_k(r), so allowed energies form bands separated by gaps. Wilson's 1931 classification used filled and empty bands to explain metals, insulators and semiconductors.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bloch, F. (1928). Über die Quantenmechanik der Elektronen in Kristallgittern. Zeitschrift für Physik, 52, 555–600.&#10;- Wilson, A. H. (1931). The theory of electronic semi-conductors. Proceedings of the Royal Society of London A, 133, 458–491.&#10;- Ashcroft, N. W., &amp; Mermin, N. D. (1976). Solid State Physics. Saunders College Publishing.">
          <outline text="Bloch theorem" _note="Periodic potentials produce Bloch waves whose crystal momentum k is a good quantum number."/>
          <outline text="Band gap and Brillouin zone" _note="Bragg scattering at zone boundaries opens gaps and organizes electronic states into bands."/>
          <outline text="Effective mass" _note="Near a band extremum, an electron responds as a free particle with mass m* set by band curvature."/>
          <outline text="Wilson metal-insulator classification" _note="Wilson (1931) distinguished metals from insulators by partially filled versus filled bands."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Semiconductors and transistor" _note="1947 · John Bardeen, Walter Brattain, William Shockley, Alan H. Wilson&#10;Doping controls electron or hole carriers, and a p–n junction rectifies current according to the Shockley diode equation I = I₀ (e^{eV/k_BT} − 1). Bardeen, Brattain and Shockley's 1947 point-contact transistor at Bell Labs launched solid-state electronics.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bardeen, J., &amp; Brattain, W. H. (1948). The transistor, a semi-conductor triode. Physical Review, 74, 230–231.&#10;- Shockley, W. (1949). The theory of p-n junctions in semiconductors and p-n junction transistors. Bell System Technical Journal, 28, 435–489.&#10;- Sze, S. M., &amp; Ng, K. K. (2007). Physics of Semiconductor Devices (3rd ed.). Wiley.">
          <outline text="Doping and carrier type" _note="Donor or acceptor impurities make n-type or p-type semiconductors with tunable conductivity."/>
          <outline text="p–n junction" _note="A depletion region and built-in field give asymmetric current–voltage rectification."/>
          <outline text="Point-contact transistor" _note="The first transistor used two closely spaced metal contacts on germanium to amplify signals."/>
          <outline text="MOSFET scaling" _note="The metal–oxide–semiconductor field-effect transistor became the scalable building block of integrated circuits."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="BCS theory" _note="1957 · John Bardeen, Leon Cooper, Robert Schrieffer&#10;Bardeen, Cooper and Schrieffer (1957) showed that a phonon-mediated attraction binds electrons into Cooper pairs, which condense into a gapped ground state. The weak-coupling gap is Δ ≈ 2ħω_D exp[−1/(N(0)V)].&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bardeen, J., Cooper, L. N., &amp; Schrieffer, J. R. (1957). Theory of superconductivity. Physical Review, 108, 1175–1204.&#10;- Tinkham, M. (1996). Introduction to Superconductivity (2nd ed.). McGraw-Hill.">
          <outline text="Cooper pairs" _note="Two electrons near the Fermi surface form a bound pair for an arbitrarily weak attractive interaction."/>
          <outline text="Phonon-mediated attraction" _note="Lattice polarization screens Coulomb repulsion and provides a retarded attraction between electrons."/>
          <outline text="BCS energy gap" _note="Breaking a pair costs energy Δ, producing an activated excitation spectrum and exponential specific heat."/>
          <outline text="Isotope effect" _note="T_c depends on ionic mass as M^{−α}, supporting the phonon mechanism."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Phase transitions and RG" _note="1970s · Kenneth G. Wilson, Leo Kadanoff, Michael E. Fisher, Lars Onsager&#10;Continuous phase transitions show power-law divergences such as ξ ∝ |T − T_c|^{−ν}, with exponents set by universality rather than microscopic details. Wilson's renormalization group (1971) explains this via fixed points of scale transformations.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Wilson, K. G. (1971). Renormalization group and critical phenomena. I. Renormalization group and the Kadanoff scaling picture. Physical Review B, 4, 3174–3183.&#10;- Wilson, K. G., &amp; Kogut, J. (1974). The renormalization group and the ε expansion. Physics Reports, 12, 75–199.&#10;- Onsager, L. (1944). Crystal statistics. I. A two-dimensional model with an order-disorder transition. Physical Review, 65, 117–149.">
          <outline text="Universality classes" _note="Systems sharing dimension and symmetry have the same critical exponents."/>
          <outline text="Scaling hypothesis" _note="Near T_c, the free energy is a homogeneous function of reduced temperature and field."/>
          <outline text="Wilson renormalization group" _note="Iterative coarse-graining flows to fixed points and yields scaling laws."/>
          <outline text="Epsilon expansion" _note="Wilson and Fisher computed exponents near four dimensions in powers of ε = 4 − d."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Quantum Hall effect" _note="1980 · Klaus von Klitzing, Horst Störmer, Daniel Tsui, Robert Laughlin&#10;In a strong perpendicular magnetic field, a two-dimensional electron gas exhibits quantized Hall conductance σ_xy = ν e²/h and vanishing longitudinal resistance. Integer (1980) and fractional (1982) plateaus arise from Landau-level filling, disorder, and topological edge states.&#10;Sources:&#10;- von Klitzing, K., Dorda, G., &amp; Pepper, M. (1980). New method for high-accuracy determination of the fine-structure constant based on quantized Hall resistance. Physical Review Letters, 45, 494–497.&#10;- Tsui, D. C., Störmer, H. L., &amp; Gossard, A. C. (1982). Two-dimensional magnetotransport in the extreme quantum limit. Physical Review Letters, 48, 1559–1562.&#10;- Laughlin, R. B. (1983). Anomalous quantum Hall effect: An incompressible quantum fluid with fractionally charged excitations. Physical Review Letters, 50, 1395–1398.">
          <outline text="Integer quantum Hall effect" _note="von Klitzing observed exact σ_xy = ν e²/h plateaus in 1980."/>
          <outline text="Fractional quantum Hall effect" _note="Tsui and Störmer observed ν = 1/3 in 1982; Laughlin's wavefunction (1983) described it."/>
          <outline text="Landau levels" _note="Cyclotron motion is quantized as E_n = ħω_c(n + 1/2)."/>
          <outline text="Topological edge states" _note="Chiral edge modes and a Chern number make the quantization robust against disorder."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="High-Tc cuprates" _note="1986 · Georg Bednorz, K. Alex Müller, Paul Chu, Hideo Hosono, Mikhail Eremets&#10;Bednorz and Müller discovered superconductivity near 35 K in La–Ba–Cu–O in 1986, and cuprates soon exceeded the 77 K boiling point of nitrogen. The pairing mechanism and pseudogap remain central open questions.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bednorz, J. G., &amp; Müller, K. A. (1986). Possible high Tc superconductivity in the Ba–La–Cu–O system. Zeitschrift für Physik B, 64, 189–193.&#10;- Wu, M. K., Ashburn, J. R., Torng, C. J., et al. (1987). Superconductivity at 93 K in a new mixed-phase Y-Ba-Cu-O compound system at ambient pressure. Physical Review Letters, 58, 908–910.&#10;- Keimer, B., Kivelson, S. A., Norman, M. R., et al. (2015). From quantum matter to high-temperature superconductivity in copper oxides. Nature, 518, 179–186.">
          <outline text="Cuprate discovery" _note="Layered copper oxides show high Tc and strong electronic anisotropy."/>
          <outline text="d-wave pairing" _note="Phase-sensitive measurements indicate a sign-changing d_{x²−y²} gap."/>
          <outline text="Pseudogap" _note="A partial gap persists above Tc, suggesting competing or precursor order."/>
          <outline text="Beyond cuprates" _note="Iron-based superconductors (Hosono, 2008) and high-pressure hydrides such as H₃S at 203 K (Eremets, 2015) extended the search for higher Tc."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Topological and 2D materials" _note="2004–present · Andre Geim, Konstantin Novoselov, Charles Kane, Eugene Mele, Pablo Jarillo-Herrero&#10;Graphene was isolated in 2004, and its low-energy electrons obey H = v_F σ·k, a massless Dirac equation. Topological invariants then classified gapped phases with protected edge states, while twisted bilayers (2018) produced flat bands and correlated phases.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Novoselov, K. S., Geim, A. K., Morozov, S. V., et al. (2004). Electric field effect in atomically thin carbon films. Science, 306, 666–669.&#10;- Kane, C. L., &amp; Mele, E. J. (2005). Quantum spin Hall effect in graphene. Physical Review Letters, 95, 226801.&#10;- Cao, Y., Fatemi, V., Fang, S., et al. (2018). Unconventional superconductivity in magic-angle graphene superlattices. Nature, 556, 43–50.">
          <outline text="Graphene Dirac cones" _note="The honeycomb lattice gives linear bands at the K and K′ points."/>
          <outline text="Topological insulators" _note="Bulk band inversion yields conducting edge or surface states protected by symmetry."/>
          <outline text="Bulk-boundary correspondence" _note="A bulk Chern or Z₂ invariant counts the protected boundary modes."/>
          <outline text="Moiré flat bands" _note="Twisted bilayer graphene hosts correlated insulators and superconductivity near the magic angle."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Astrophysics and cosmology" _note="1915-present · General relativity, Hubble's law and the cosmic microwave background established an expanding universe with dark matter and dark energy.">
        <outline text="Stellar structure and nucleosynthesis" _note="1920s-1957 · Arthur Eddington, Hans Bethe, Fred Hoyle, Margaret Burbidge, Geoffrey Burbidge, William Fowler&#10;Eddington's 1926 models treated stars as gas spheres in hydrostatic equilibrium, with energy carried outward by radiation, and Bethe identified the fusion reactions that power them in 1939. The 1957 B²FH paper explained how stars synthesize elements up to iron and, via neutron capture, beyond it.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Eddington, A. S. (1926). The Internal Constitution of the Stars. Cambridge University Press.&#10;- Bethe, H. A. (1939). Energy production in stars. Physical Review, 55, 434-456.&#10;- Burbidge, E. M., Burbidge, G. R., Fowler, W. A., et al. (1957). Synthesis of the elements in stars. Reviews of Modern Physics, 29, 547-650.">
          <outline text="Hydrostatic equilibrium" _note="Gas pressure gradients balance gravity in a stable star, dP/dr = -Gm(r)ρ/r²."/>
          <outline text="Eddington luminosity" _note="Radiation pressure sets a maximum stable luminosity, L_Edd = 4πGMm_p c/σ_T."/>
          <outline text="Hydrogen fusion chains" _note="The pp chain and CNO cycle convert hydrogen to helium and power main-sequence stars."/>
          <outline text="B²FH stellar nucleosynthesis" _note="The 1957 B²FH paper built elements up to iron by fusion, with heavier nuclei made by slow neutron capture (s-process) in stars and rapid neutron capture (r-process) in explosive events; the 2017 kilonova GW170817 confirmed r-process production in neutron-star mergers."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Hubble law" _note="1929 · Edwin Hubble, Georges Lemaître, Milton Humason&#10;Hubble's 1929 observations showed that galaxy recession velocity grows linearly with distance, v = H₀d, evidence for an expanding universe. Lemaître had derived the relation and its expansion interpretation two years earlier.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Hubble, E. (1929). A relation between distance and radial velocity among extra-galactic nebulae. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 15, 168-173.&#10;- Lemaître, G. (1927). Un univers homogène de masse constante et de rayon croissant rendant compte de la vitesse radiale des nébuleuses extra-galactiques [A homogeneous universe of constant mass and increasing radius accounting for the radial velocity of extra-galactic nebulae]. Annales de la Société Scientifique de Bruxelles, 47, 49-59.">
          <outline text="Hubble constant H₀" _note="The present expansion rate, measured near 70 km/s/Mpc, with a persistent discrepancy between distance-ladder and early-universe determinations."/>
          <outline text="Distance ladder" _note="Cepheid variables and standard candles calibrate extragalactic distances step by step."/>
          <outline text="Lemaître's expanding universe" _note="In 1927 Lemaître derived v = H₀d and interpreted it as cosmic expansion, before Hubble's paper."/>
          <outline text="Redshift-distance relation" _note="Cosmological redshift of galaxy spectra grows with distance, a signature of metric expansion."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Dark matter evidence" _note="1933-1970s · Fritz Zwicky, Vera Rubin, Kent Ford, Jan Oort&#10;Zwicky (1933) found that galaxies in the Coma cluster move too fast for its visible mass, and Rubin and Ford (1970s) measured flat rotation curves in spiral galaxies. Both imply large amounts of non-luminous matter, with v² = GM(r)/r requiring M(r) ∝ r at large radii.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Zwicky, F. (1933). Die Rotverschiebung von extragalaktischen Nebeln [The redshift of extragalactic nebulae]. Helvetica Physica Acta, 6, 110-127.&#10;- Rubin, V. C., &amp; Ford, W. K. (1970). Rotation of the Andromeda nebula from a spectroscopic survey of emission regions. Astrophysical Journal, 159, 379-403.&#10;- Clowe, D., Bradač, M., Gonzalez, A. H., et al. (2006). A direct empirical proof of the existence of dark matter. Astrophysical Journal Letters, 648, L109-L113.">
          <outline text="Coma cluster mass discrepancy" _note="Zwicky inferred far more cluster mass than visible light suggested from galaxy velocity dispersions."/>
          <outline text="Flat rotation curves" _note="Orbital speeds stay roughly constant at large galactic radii, implying dark matter halos."/>
          <outline text="Mass-to-light ratios" _note="Galaxies and clusters show M/L far above values expected from stars alone."/>
          <outline text="Bullet Cluster" _note="In 2006 gravitational lensing showed the mass concentrated apart from the hot shocked gas in the colliding cluster 1E 0657-56, strong direct evidence for dark matter."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Compact stars and black holes" _note="1930s-2019 · Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar, Lev Landau, J. Robert Oppenheimer, Roger Penrose, Stephen Hawking, Jocelyn Bell Burnell, Antony Hewish, Reinhard Genzel, Andrea Ghez, Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration&#10;Chandrasekhar's 1931 limit showed that white dwarfs above about 1.4 solar masses must collapse, and Oppenheimer and Snyder (1939) modeled continued collapse to a black hole. The Event Horizon Telescope imaged the shadow of M87* in 2019, confirming the black-hole picture.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Chandrasekhar, S. (1931). The maximum mass of ideal white dwarfs. Astrophysical Journal, 74, 81-82.&#10;- Oppenheimer, J. R., &amp; Snyder, H. (1939). On continued gravitational contraction. Physical Review, 56, 455-459.&#10;- Event Horizon Telescope Collaboration, Akiyama, K., Alberdi, A., et al. (2019). First M87 Event Horizon Telescope results. I. The shadow of the supermassive black hole. Astrophysical Journal Letters, 875, L1.">
          <outline text="Chandrasekhar limit" _note="White dwarfs above roughly 1.4 M_sun cannot be supported by electron degeneracy pressure."/>
          <outline text="Neutron stars and pulsars" _note="Jocelyn Bell Burnell and Antony Hewish discovered pulsars in 1967, confirming neutron stars supported by degeneracy pressure."/>
          <outline text="Oppenheimer-Snyder collapse" _note="The first relativistic model of gravitational collapse forming a black hole."/>
          <outline text="Event Horizon Telescope" _note="Its 2019 radio image of M87*'s shadow gave direct evidence for a supermassive black hole, followed by an image of Sgr A* in 2022."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Big Bang nucleosynthesis" _note="1948 · George Gamow, Ralph Alpher, Robert Herman&#10;Alpher, Bethe and Gamow (1948) showed that the hot early universe synthesized light nuclei, predicting roughly 25% helium by mass. Alpher and Herman also predicted the relic radiation left over from that epoch.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Alpher, R. A., Bethe, H., &amp; Gamow, G. (1948). The origin of chemical elements. Physical Review, 73, 803-804.&#10;- Alpher, R. A., &amp; Herman, R. C. (1948). Evolution of the universe. Nature, 162, 774-775.&#10;- Fields, B. D., Olive, K. A., Yeh, T.-H., et al. (2020). Big-Bang nucleosynthesis after Planck. Journal of Cosmology and Astroparticle Physics, 2020(03), 010.">
          <outline text="Alpher-Bethe-Gamow paper" _note="The 1948 calculation of primordial element abundances in a hot Big Bang."/>
          <outline text="Neutron-proton freeze-out" _note="Weak interactions decouple near 1 MeV, fixing the neutron-to-proton ratio at about 1/7."/>
          <outline text="Deuterium bottleneck" _note="Deuterium formation waits until the temperature falls to about 0.1 MeV, starting nucleosynthesis."/>
          <outline text="Primordial abundances" _note="Predicted H, D, ³He, ⁴He and ⁷Li abundances broadly match observation, with a lingering lithium problem."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Cosmic microwave background" _note="1965 · Arno Penzias, Robert Wilson, Robert Dicke, Jim Peebles, John Mather, George Smoot&#10;Penzias and Wilson detected an unexpected isotropic 2.7 K microwave background in 1965, quickly interpreted by Dicke's group as relic radiation from the hot Big Bang. COBE's FIRAS measured its blackbody spectrum in 1990 and its DMR found the tiny anisotropies in 1992 that seed structure.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Penzias, A. A., &amp; Wilson, R. W. (1965). A measurement of excess antenna temperature at 4080 Mc/s. Astrophysical Journal, 142, 419-421.&#10;- Dicke, R. H., Peebles, P. J. E., Roll, P. G., et al. (1965). Cosmic black-body radiation. Astrophysical Journal, 142, 414-419.&#10;- Smoot, G. F., Bennett, C. L., Kogut, A., et al. (1992). Structure in the COBE differential microwave radiometer first-year maps. Astrophysical Journal Letters, 396, L1-L5.">
          <outline text="Penzias-Wilson discovery" _note="The accidental 1965 detection of isotropic 2.7 K microwave noise at Bell Labs."/>
          <outline text="COBE blackbody spectrum" _note="The 1990 FIRAS measurement matched a Planck spectrum to extraordinary precision, T = 2.725 K."/>
          <outline text="CMB acoustic peaks" _note="WMAP and Planck map temperature anisotropies and pin down cosmological parameters."/>
          <outline text="Recombination and decoupling" _note="Neutral atoms formed near redshift z ≈ 1100, letting photons free-stream to us."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Cosmic inflation" _note="1980s · Alexei Starobinsky, Alan Guth, Andrei Linde, Paul Steinhardt, Andreas Albrecht&#10;Starobinsky (1980) and Guth (1981) proposed a brief exponential expansion that solves the horizon and flatness problems and seeds cosmic structure. Linde, Albrecht and Steinhardt developed the slow-roll variants in 1982.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Starobinsky, A. A. (1980). A new type of isotropic cosmological models without singularity. Physics Letters B, 91, 99-102.&#10;- Guth, A. H. (1981). Inflationary universe: A possible solution to the horizon and flatness problems. Physical Review D, 23, 347-356.&#10;- Linde, A. D. (1982). A new inflationary universe scenario: A possible solution of the horizon, flatness, homogeneity, isotropy and primordial monopole problems. Physics Letters B, 108, 389-393.">
          <outline text="Horizon problem" _note="Regions of the CMB farther apart than the causal horizon nonetheless share the same temperature."/>
          <outline text="Flatness problem" _note="Inflation drives the density parameter Ω toward 1, explaining the near-flat geometry."/>
          <outline text="Slow-roll inflation" _note="A scalar field rolling slowly gives near-exponential expansion, a(t) ∝ e^{Ht}, and density perturbations."/>
          <outline text="Primordial B-modes" _note="An open search for inflationary gravitational waves imprinted on CMB polarization."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Dark energy and ΛCDM" _note="1998-2018 · Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt, Adam Riess, Dark Energy Survey Collaboration, Planck Collaboration&#10;In 1998 two supernova teams found distant Type Ia supernovae fainter than expected, implying accelerating expansion driven by dark energy with equation of state w ≈ -1. ΛCDM now fits CMB, supernova and BAO data, although local and early-universe H₀ measurements disagree.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Riess, A. G., Filippenko, A. V., Challis, P., et al. (1998). Observational evidence from supernovae for an accelerating universe and a cosmological constant. Astronomical Journal, 116, 1009-1038.&#10;- Perlmutter, S., Aldering, G., Goldhaber, G., et al. (1999). Measurements of Ω and Λ from 42 high-redshift supernovae. Astrophysical Journal, 517, 565-586.&#10;- Planck Collaboration, Aghanim, N., Akrami, Y., et al. (2020). Planck 2018 results. VI. Cosmological parameters. Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics, 641, A6.">
          <outline text="Accelerating universe (1998)" _note="High-redshift Type Ia supernovae reveal that cosmic expansion is speeding up."/>
          <outline text="Cosmological constant Λ" _note="Vacuum energy with negative pressure, obeying w = p/ρc² ≈ -1, drives the acceleration."/>
          <outline text="ΛCDM model" _note="A six-parameter model with cold dark matter and Λ fits CMB, BAO and supernova data, H² = 8πGρ/3 + Λc²/3 - kc²/a²."/>
          <outline text="Baryon acoustic oscillations" _note="The 2005 SDSS and 2dFGRS detections of the ~150 Mpc BAO feature provided a standard ruler for mapping the expansion history."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Fluids, plasmas and complex systems" _note="1822-present · Continuum flows, plasmas, chaos and soft and living matter show how collective, nonlinear behaviour emerges from simple laws.">
        <outline text="Navier-Stokes equations" _note="1822-1845 · Claude-Louis Navier, Augustin-Louis Cauchy, Siméon Denis Poisson, George Gabriel Stokes&#10;These equations express momentum balance for a viscous Newtonian fluid, combining inertia with pressure and viscous stress: ρ(∂u/∂t + u·∇u) = −∇p + μ∇²u + f, with ∇·u = 0 for incompressible flow. Navier (1822) and Stokes (1845) introduced the viscous term through Cauchy's stress tensor.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Navier, C.-L. (1827). Mémoire sur les lois du mouvement des fluides [Memoir on the laws of fluid motion; read 1822]. Mémoires de l'Académie Royale des Sciences de l'Institut de France, 6, 389-440.&#10;- Stokes, G. G. (1845). On the theories of the internal friction of fluids in motion, and of the equilibrium and motion of elastic solids. Transactions of the Cambridge Philosophical Society, 8, 287-305.&#10;- Landau, L. D., &amp; Lifshitz, E. M. (1987). Fluid Mechanics (2nd ed., Course of Theoretical Physics, Vol. 6). Pergamon Press.">
          <outline text="Cauchy stress tensor" _note="Cauchy's 1822 stress principle represents surface traction as a tensor, which yields the viscous term μ∇²u in the momentum equation."/>
          <outline text="Stokes flow and drag" _note="When inertia is negligible the equations linearize, giving Stokes flow and the drag law F = 6πμRv for a sphere (1851)."/>
          <outline text="No-slip boundary condition" _note="Matching fluid velocity to solid walls closes the boundary-value problem, though slip appears in rarefied, microfluidic and superhydrophobic regimes."/>
          <outline text="Existence and smoothness (open)" _note="Whether smooth three-dimensional solutions exist for all time and all smooth data is an unsolved Clay Millennium Prize problem (2000)."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Turbulence" _note="1883-1941 · Osborne Reynolds, Ludwig Prandtl, Geoffrey Ingram Taylor, Theodore von Kármán, Andrey Kolmogorov&#10;Above a critical Reynolds number flow becomes chaotic and energy cascades from large eddies to small ones where viscosity dissipates it. Kolmogorov's 1941 theory predicts the inertial-range spectrum E(k) ∝ ε^(2/3) k^(−5/3).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Reynolds, O. (1883). An experimental investigation of the circumstances which determine whether the motion of water shall be direct or sinuous, and of the law of resistance in parallel channels. Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London, 174, 935-982.&#10;- Kolmogorov, A. N. (1941). The local structure of turbulence in incompressible viscous fluid for very large Reynolds numbers. Doklady Akademii Nauk SSSR, 30, 301-305.&#10;- Frisch, U. (1995). Turbulence: The Legacy of A. N. Kolmogorov. Cambridge University Press.">
          <outline text="Reynolds number" _note="Reynolds' 1883 pipe experiments showed laminar flow gives way to turbulence near Re = ρUL/μ ≈ 2000."/>
          <outline text="Kolmogorov −5/3 spectrum" _note="Kolmogorov's similarity hypotheses (1941) give E(k) ∝ k^(−5/3) and the exact four-fifths law for third-order structure functions."/>
          <outline text="Boundary-layer theory" _note="Prandtl (1904) showed viscous effects confine to thin layers near walls, reconciling ideal-flow theory with observed drag and separation."/>
          <outline text="Closure problem" _note="Averaging the equations generates unknown velocity correlations, so RANS and large-eddy models remain partly empirical — an open modeling question."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Plasma physics and MHD" _note="1920s-1940s · Irving Langmuir, Peter Debye, Hannes Alfvén, Lev Landau, Sydney Chapman&#10;In ionized gas, long-range Coulomb forces make particles act collectively over the Debye length, supporting waves and instabilities. Ideal magnetohydrodynamics couples fluid momentum to Maxwell's fields: ρ(∂u/∂t + u·∇u) = J×B − ∇p with ∂B/∂t = ∇×(u×B).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Langmuir, I. (1928). Oscillations in ionized gases. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the USA, 14, 627-637.&#10;- Alfvén, H. (1942). Existence of electromagnetic-hydrodynamic waves. Nature, 150, 405-406.&#10;- Debye, P., &amp; Hückel, E. (1923). Zur Theorie der Elektrolyte. I. Gefrierpunktserniedrigung und verwandte Erscheinungen [On the theory of electrolytes. I. Freezing point depression and related phenomena]. Physikalische Zeitschrift, 24, 185-206.">
          <outline text="Debye shielding" _note="Debye and Hückel (1923) gave the screening length λ_D = √(ε₀kT/ne²), the scale over which a plasma neutralizes charge imbalances."/>
          <outline text="Plasma oscillations" _note="Langmuir (1928) found collective electron oscillations at ω_p = √(ne²/ε₀m) and coined the term 'plasma' for the ionized state."/>
          <outline text="Alfvén waves" _note="Alfvén (1942) predicted transverse waves propagating along field lines at v_A = B/√(μ₀ρ), the cornerstone of MHD."/>
          <outline text="Magnetic reconnection" _note="Field lines break and rejoin, releasing magnetic energy in solar flares and tokamak disruptions; the fast reconnection rate remains debated."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Chaos" _note="1890-1963 · Henri Poincaré, Edward Lorenz, Stephen Smale, Andrey Kolmogorov, Vladimir Arnold, Jürgen Moser&#10;Nonlinear deterministic systems can display sensitive dependence on initial conditions, so nearby trajectories separate exponentially and long-term prediction fails. Poincaré found the geometry in the three-body problem (1890) and Lorenz exhibited it in a truncated convection model (1963).&#10;Sources:&#10;- Poincaré, H. (1890). Sur le problème des trois corps et les équations de la dynamique [On the three-body problem and the equations of dynamics]. Acta Mathematica, 13, 1-270.&#10;- Lorenz, E. N. (1963). Deterministic nonperiodic flow. Journal of the Atmospheric Sciences, 20, 130-141.&#10;- Ott, E. (2002). Chaos in Dynamical Systems (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press.">
          <outline text="Sensitive dependence" _note="A positive Lyapunov exponent makes small errors grow as δ(t) ≈ δ₀e^(λt), setting a finite predictability horizon."/>
          <outline text="Lorenz attractor" _note="Lorenz's 1963 three-variable convection equations produce a strange attractor of fractal geometry, popularized as the butterfly effect."/>
          <outline text="Homoclinic tangles" _note="Poincaré's 1890 work on the three-body problem found intersecting stable and unstable manifolds, a geometric mechanism for chaos."/>
          <outline text="KAM theorem" _note="Kolmogorov (1954) with Arnold and Moser (1962-63) proved most invariant tori survive small perturbations, delimiting where chaos appears."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Solitons" _note='1965 · Norman Zabusky, Martin Kruskal, John Scott Russell, Diederik Korteweg, Gustav de Vries&#10;A soliton is a localized wave that survives collisions unchanged because nonlinear steepening exactly balances dispersion. Zabusky and Kruskal coined the name in 1965 while numerically studying the Korteweg–de Vries equation u_t + u u_x + δ² u_xxx = 0.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Zabusky, N. J., &amp; Kruskal, M. D. (1965). Interaction of "solitons" in a collisionless plasma and the recurrence of initial states. Physical Review Letters, 15, 240-243.&#10;- Korteweg, D. J., &amp; de Vries, G. (1895). On the change of form of long waves advancing in a rectangular canal, and on a new type of long stationary waves. Philosophical Magazine, 39, 422-443.&#10;- Russell, J. S. (1844). Report on waves. Report of the Fourteenth Meeting of the British Association for the Advancement of Science, 311-390.'>
          <outline text="Wave of translation" _note="John Scott Russell observed in 1834 a solitary water wave that traveled for miles without changing shape."/>
          <outline text="Korteweg-de Vries equation" _note="Korteweg and de Vries (1895) derived u_t + u u_x + δ²u_xxx = 0, balancing nonlinearity against dispersion."/>
          <outline text="Inverse scattering transform" _note="Gardner, Greene, Kruskal and Miura (1967) solved KdV by mapping it to a linear scattering problem, revealing infinitely many conservation laws."/>
          <outline text="Optical solitons" _note="Hasegawa and Tappert (1973) predicted nonlinear Schrödinger solitons in optical fibers, now exploited in long-haul communications."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Soft matter and biophysics" _note="1950s-present · Paul Flory, Pierre-Gilles de Gennes, Sam Edwards, Masao Doi, Lars Onsager&#10;Soft matter is governed by thermal energy kT and entropy, so weak interactions and Brownian motion set mesoscopic structure. Canonical results include polymer scaling R_F ~ N^(3/5)a and reptation dynamics with relaxation time τ ~ N³.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Flory, P. J. (1953). Principles of Polymer Chemistry. Cornell University Press.&#10;- de Gennes, P.-G. (1979). Scaling Concepts in Polymer Physics. Cornell University Press.&#10;- Doi, M., &amp; Edwards, S. F. (1986). The Theory of Polymer Dynamics. Oxford University Press.">
          <outline text="Flory-Huggins theory" _note="Flory (1941) and Huggins described polymer solutions and blends with a lattice free energy governed by the interaction parameter χ."/>
          <outline text="Reptation and tube model" _note="de Gennes (1971) and Doi-Edwards (1978) model entangled chains sliding in a tube, giving τ ~ N³ stress relaxation."/>
          <outline text="Liquid crystals" _note="Onsager (1949) and de Gennes explained orientational order and its nematic-isotropic transitions in rod-like and molecular materials."/>
          <outline text="Single-molecule biophysics" _note="Optical tweezers, FRET and cryo-EM since the 1990s measure forces and conformational changes in individual proteins, DNA and molecular motors."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Self-organized criticality and networks" _note="1987-1999 · Per Bak, Chao Tang, Kurt Wiesenfeld, Duncan Watts, Steven Strogatz, Albert-László Barabási&#10;Slowly driven dissipative systems can organize themselves into a critical state without external tuning, yielding power-law avalanche statistics P(s) ~ s^(−τ). Network science then showed that topology, from small-world to scale-free structure, controls collective behaviour.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bak, P., Tang, C., &amp; Wiesenfeld, K. (1987). Self-organized criticality: An explanation of 1/f noise. Physical Review Letters, 59, 381-384.&#10;- Watts, D. J., &amp; Strogatz, S. H. (1998). Collective dynamics of 'small-world' networks. Nature, 393, 440-442.&#10;- Barabási, A.-L., &amp; Albert, R. (1999). Emergence of scaling in random networks. Science, 286, 509-512.">
          <outline text="Sandpile model" _note="Bak, Tang and Wiesenfeld (1987) showed a cellular-automaton sandpile drives itself to a critical slope with power-law avalanche sizes."/>
          <outline text="1/f noise and power laws" _note="Critical states generate scale-free fluctuations, linking earthquakes, forest fires and neuronal avalanches to a common statistical signature."/>
          <outline text="Small-world networks" _note="Watts and Strogatz (1998) found that a few random rewires sharply reduce path length while preserving high clustering."/>
          <outline text="Scale-free networks" _note="Barabási and Albert (1999) showed preferential attachment yields P(k) ~ k^(−3), so a few hubs dominate robustness and spreading."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Open frontier" _note="today · The biggest open questions and most active research programmes in physics as of 2026.">
        <outline text="Quantum gravity" _note="roots 1915–1930s · Albert Einstein, Stephen Hawking, Juan Maldacena, Abhay Ashtekar, Carlo Rovelli, Martin Reuter · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: general relativity and quantum field theory conflict at the Planck scale ℓ_P = √(ħG/c³) ≈ 1.6×10⁻³⁵ m, while holography supplies consistent toy models. Unknown: a background-independent, empirically confirmed 4D theory; string theory/AdS-CFT, loop quantum gravity, asymptotic safety and causal dynamical triangulation are the main programmes, and no decisive experiment exists—LIGO's 2015 detection only opened strong-field tests.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Hawking, S. W. (1975). Particle creation by black holes. Communications in Mathematical Physics, 43, 199–220.&#10;- Maldacena, J. M. (1998). The large N limit of superconformal field theories and supergravity. Advances in Theoretical and Mathematical Physics, 2, 231–252.&#10;- Ashtekar, A., &amp; Lewandowski, J. (2004). Background independent quantum gravity: A status report. Classical and Quantum Gravity, 21, R53–R152.&#10;- Almheiri, A., Hartman, T., Maldacena, J., et al. (2021). The entropy of Hawking radiation. Reviews of Modern Physics, 93, 035002.">
          <outline text="Holographic duality" _note="Maldacena's 1997 AdS/CFT correspondence equates a gravity theory in anti-de Sitter space with a boundary conformal field theory, giving the best-defined nonperturbative quantum-gravity models."/>
          <outline text="Black-hole information paradox" _note="Hawking's 1974 calculation suggested information loss; 2019 replica-wormhole derivations of the Page curve support unitarity in holographic models, but the real-world resolution is open."/>
          <outline text="Loop quantum gravity" _note="Ashtekar's 1986 variables and Rovelli–Smolin spin networks quantize geometry; deriving a 4D continuum limit and low-energy gravity remains open."/>
          <outline text="Planck-scale phenomenology" _note="Gamma-ray-burst and gravitational-wave data constrain Lorentz violation and extra dimensions, but no Planck-scale signal has been confirmed."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Dark matter identity" _note="since 1933 · Fritz Zwicky, Vera Rubin, Elena Aprile, Laura Baudis, Katherine Freese · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: about 27% of the cosmic energy density is cold, collisionless dark matter, inferred from galaxy rotation, lensing, the CMB and structure formation (Ω_DM h² ≈ 0.12). Unknown: its identity; LZ's 2024 null result reached about 2×10⁻⁴⁸ cm² near 40 GeV, and the 2024 XENONnT and PandaX-4T detection of solar ⁸B neutrinos via coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering marks the neutrino fog now limiting WIMP searches.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Bertone, G., &amp; Hooper, D. (2018). History of dark matter. Reviews of Modern Physics, 90, 045002.&#10;- Aalbers, J., et al. (LZ Collaboration) (2025). Dark matter search results from 4.2 tonne-years of exposure of the LUX-ZEPLIN (LZ) experiment. Physical Review Letters, 135, 011802.&#10;- Aprile, E., et al. (XENON Collaboration) (2024). First indication of solar 8B neutrinos via coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering with XENONnT. Physical Review Letters, 133, 191002.">
          <outline text="WIMP direct detection" _note="Xenon and argon detectors see no nuclear recoils above background; LZ (2024) reached about 2×10⁻⁴⁸ cm² near 40 GeV, pushing WIMP–nucleon limits into the neutrino-fog regime."/>
          <outline text="Axion haloscopes" _note="ADMX and HAYSTAC scan microwave cavities for axion–photon conversion; only small mass windows are excluded."/>
          <outline text="Neutrino fog" _note="In 2024 XENONnT and PandaX-4T detected solar ⁸B neutrinos through coherent elastic neutrino-nucleus scattering, the background floor that WIMP searches are now reaching."/>
          <outline text="MOND alternatives" _note="Milgrom's 1983 modified-gravity fits galaxy curves but struggles with clusters and the CMB; no alternative is accepted."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Dark energy and Hubble tension" _note="discovery 1998 · Saul Perlmutter, Brian Schmidt, Adam Riess, Wendy Freedman, Licia Verde · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: cosmic expansion accelerates, and ΛCDM fits CMB, BAO and supernovae with dark energy ~68% and w = p/(ρc²) ≈ −1; the decisive discovery was the 1998 supernova result. Unknown: whether w evolves and whether the Hubble tension is new physics or systematics; DESI DR2 (March 2025) combined with CMB and supernovae prefers evolving dark energy at 2.8–4.2σ, below discovery, DESI completed its five-year survey in April 2026 with final results expected in 2027, and JWST/CCHP estimates near H₀ ≈ 70 km/s/Mpc make the tension debated.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Riess, A. G., Filippenko, A. V., Challis, P., et al. (1998). Observational evidence from supernovae for an accelerating universe and a cosmological constant. Astronomical Journal, 116, 1009–1038.&#10;- Riess, A. G., Yuan, W., Macri, L. M., et al. (2022). A comprehensive measurement of the local value of the Hubble constant with 1 km/s/Mpc uncertainty from the Hubble Space Telescope and the SH0ES team. Astrophysical Journal Letters, 934, L7.&#10;- Abdul-Karim, M., et al. (DESI Collaboration) (2025). DESI DR2 results. II. Measurements of baryon acoustic oscillations and cosmological constraints. Physical Review D, 112, 083515.">
          <outline text="Cosmic acceleration" _note="Riess/Perlmutter 1998 found distant SNe Ia dimmer than expected; Λ has w = −1."/>
          <outline text="Hubble tension" _note="SH0ES gives H₀ ≈ 73 and Planck ≈ 67.4 km/s/Mpc, while Freedman's JWST/CCHP ladder gives ≈ 70; whether the discrepancy is new physics or systematics is debated."/>
          <outline text="DESI evolving dark energy" _note="DESI DR2 (March 2025) with CMB and supernovae favours w0waCDM over Λ at 2.8–4.2σ, below the 5σ discovery threshold; the five-year survey finished in April 2026 and final results are expected in 2027."/>
          <outline text="Euclid and Rubin" _note="Euclid (launched 2023) and Rubin/LSST, which released its first images in 2025, will map weak lensing and BAO to test w(z) and the Hubble tension."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Beyond the Standard Model" _note="since 1960s–1970s · Peter Higgs, François Englert, Fabiola Gianotti, Gian Giudice, Hitoshi Murayama · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: the Standard Model gauge group SU(3)×SU(2)×U(1) fits collider data, and the 2012 Higgs discovery completed its particle content; it still cannot explain neutrino masses, dark matter, baryon asymmetry, the hierarchy or gravity. Unknown: which extension is realized and at what scale; the LHC has found no SUSY or extra dimensions, Fermilab's final 2025 muon g-2 result agrees with lattice-based theory so the anomaly has largely gone, R(K) and R(K*) returned to Standard Model values in 2022 while R(D)/R(D*) still show ~3σ tension, and searches continue at HL-LHC, Belle II, LHCb, EDM and dark-sector experiments.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Navas, S., et al. (Particle Data Group) (2024). Review of Particle Physics. Physical Review D, 110, 030001.&#10;- ATLAS Collaboration, Aad, G., et al. (2012). Observation of a new particle in the search for the Standard Model Higgs boson with the ATLAS detector at the LHC. Physics Letters B, 716, 1–29.&#10;- Aguillard, D. P., et al. (Muon g-2 Collaboration) (2025). Measurement of the positive muon anomalous magnetic moment to 127 ppb. Physical Review Letters, 135, 101802.">
          <outline text="Higgs boson discovery" _note="ATLAS and CMS found a ~125 GeV scalar in 2012; its couplings match the Standard Model, while the hierarchy problem remains open."/>
          <outline text="LHC null results" _note="No supersymmetric particles or extra dimensions have appeared; gluino and squark limits reach the multi-TeV scale."/>
          <outline text="Flavor anomalies" _note="LHCb's R(K) and R(K*) moved back to Standard Model values in December 2022, but R(D)/R(D*) still show a tension of about 3σ."/>
          <outline text="Muon g-2 resolution" _note="Fermilab published its final result in June 2025 at 127 ppb precision; the 2025 Theory Initiative lattice-QCD prediction agrees, so the anomaly has largely gone, leaving a disagreement between lattice and data-driven estimates of hadronic vacuum polarization."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Neutrino masses and nature" _note="oscillations 1998–2002 · Bruno Pontecorvo, Takaaki Kajita, Arthur McDonald, Koichiro Nishikawa, Kate Scholberg, Francis Halzen · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: neutrinos oscillate, so at least two masses are nonzero; Super-Kamiokande (1998) and SNO (2001) established atmospheric and solar oscillations, and mixing angles and Δm² are measured. Unknown: absolute mass scale, ordering, Dirac versus Majorana nature and leptonic CP violation; KATRIN's 2025 limit is mβ &lt; 0.45 eV, cosmology (DESI 2025 + CMB) gives Σmν below about 0.07 eV near the ~0.06 eV oscillation minimum, JUNO's June 2026 first results give world-leading solar-parameter precision but not the ordering, global fits mildly favour normal ordering (~2.2σ), and JUNO, DUNE, KATRIN and neutrinoless double-beta experiments target the remaining questions.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Fukuda, Y., Hayakawa, T., Ichihara, E., et al. (1998). Evidence for oscillation of atmospheric neutrinos. Physical Review Letters, 81, 1562–1567.&#10;- Ahmad, Q. R., Allen, R. C., Andersen, T. C., et al. (2001). Measurement of the rate of νe + d → p + p + e⁻ interactions produced by 8B solar neutrinos at the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory. Physical Review Letters, 87, 071301.&#10;- Aker, M., et al. (KATRIN Collaboration) (2025). Direct neutrino-mass measurement based on 259 days of KATRIN data. Science, 388, 180–185.&#10;- JUNO Collaboration (2026). Measurement of reactor neutrino oscillation with the first JUNO data. Nature, 654, 343–348.&#10;- IceCube Collaboration, Aartsen, M. G., et al. (2013). Evidence for high-energy extraterrestrial neutrinos at the IceCube detector. Science, 342, 1242856.">
          <outline text="Neutrino oscillation" _note="The PMNS formula P(νe→νμ) = sin²2θ sin²(Δm²L/4E) fits solar, atmospheric, reactor and beam data."/>
          <outline text="Mass ordering" _note="JUNO's 2026 first results give world-leading precision on sin²θ₁₂ and Δm²₂₁ but not the ordering; global fits mildly favour normal ordering at about 2.2σ."/>
          <outline text="Majorana versus Dirac" _note="Neutrinoless double-beta searches (CUORE, LEGEND, KamLAND-Zen) have seen no signal, so whether the neutrino is its own antiparticle is open."/>
          <outline text="Absolute neutrino mass" _note="KATRIN's 2025 limit is mβ &lt; 0.45 eV, while cosmology gives Σmν below about 0.07 eV; the absolute scale remains unknown."/>
          <outline text="Neutrino astronomy" _note="IceCube's discovery of high-energy astrophysical neutrinos earned Francis Halzen the 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics, opening a new window on cosmic accelerators."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Strongly correlated quantum matter" _note="cuprates 1986 · Philip Anderson, Nevill Mott, Pablo Jarillo-Herrero, Subir Sachdev, Ali Yazdani · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: strong electron correlations produce Mott insulators, unconventional superconductivity, fractional quantum anomalous Hall states and quantum spin liquids, often described by Hubbard-type models H = −tΣ c†c + UΣ n↑n↓. Unknown: the cuprate pairing mechanism and pseudogap, whether the Hubbard model itself superconducts, strange-metal transport and how to design higher-temperature superconductors; ARPES, STM, neutron scattering, cold atoms and moiré systems are the main probes, with fractional quantum anomalous Hall states in twisted MoTe₂ and rhombohedral graphene (2023–2024) a recent decisive development.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Lee, P. A., Nagaosa, N., &amp; Wen, X.-G. (2006). Doping a Mott insulator: Physics of high-temperature superconductivity. Reviews of Modern Physics, 78, 17–85.&#10;- Cao, Y., Fatemi, V., Fang, S., et al. (2018). Unconventional superconductivity in magic-angle graphene superlattices. Nature, 556, 43–50.&#10;- Park, H., Cai, J., Anderson, E., et al. (2023). Observation of fractionally quantized anomalous Hall effect. Nature, 622, 74–79.&#10;- Lu, Z., Han, T., Yao, Y., et al. (2024). Fractional quantum anomalous Hall effect in multilayer graphene. Nature, 626, 759–764.">
          <outline text="Cuprate pairing puzzle" _note="Whether cuprates are captured by Hubbard or t–J models, what causes the pseudogap, and what mediates pairing remain unresolved despite decades of ARPES, STM and neutron data."/>
          <outline text="Magic-angle graphene" _note="Twisted bilayer graphene at the magic angle (2018) shows correlated insulators and superconductivity, a tunable flat-band laboratory."/>
          <outline text="Strange metal" _note="Cuprates and heavy fermions show resistivity linear in T down to low temperature, violating Fermi-liquid expectations; its origin is open."/>
          <outline text="Fractional quantum anomalous Hall" _note="In 2023–2024 fractional Hall states appeared without an applied magnetic field in twisted MoTe₂ and rhombohedral graphene, creating a new platform for anyons and topological order."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Quantum computing and simulation" _note="proposal 1982 · Richard Feynman, Peter Shor, David Deutsch, John Martinis, Michel Devoret, Christopher Monroe, Mikhail Lukin · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: qubits support high-fidelity gates and small surface-code logical qubits now suppress errors as distance grows, with Google's 2024 Willow result operating below threshold; the 2025 Nobel Prize (Clarke, Devoret, Martinis) honoured macroscopic quantum effects in electrical circuits, the basis of superconducting qubits. Unknown: a large fault-tolerant machine with verified practical advantage; Google's 2025 Quantum Echoes out-of-time-order-correlator experiment claims a verifiable speed-up but practical advantage is debated, and superconducting, trapped-ion, neutral-atom and photonic platforms all face error-rate, scaling and verification challenges.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Feynman, R. P. (1982). Simulating physics with computers. International Journal of Theoretical Physics, 21, 467–488.&#10;- Shor, P. W. (1997). Polynomial-time algorithms for prime factorization and discrete logarithms on a quantum computer. SIAM Journal on Computing, 26, 1484–1509.&#10;- Google Quantum AI and Collaborators (2025). Quantum error correction below the surface code threshold. Nature, 638, 920–926.">
          <outline text="Quantum advantage" _note="Google's 2025 Quantum Echoes out-of-time-order-correlator experiment claims a verifiable speed-up; practical advantage remains debated."/>
          <outline text="Surface-code threshold" _note="Below the threshold p &lt; p_th, logical error falls exponentially with code distance; Willow demonstrated this in 2024 but with small logical qubits."/>
          <outline text="Neutral-atom arrays" _note="Harvard/QuEra ran 48 logical qubits in 2023 and Caltech trapped about 6,100 atoms in one array in 2025, but gate fidelity at scale remains the challenge."/>
          <outline text="Quantum simulation" _note="Feynman's 1982 proposal underlies analog and digital simulation of materials and chemistry; a clear beyond-classical useful result is still open."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Controlled fusion energy" _note="since 1950s · Andrei Sakharov, Igor Tamm, John Lawson, Lyman Spitzer · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: D–T fusion can exceed target gain, with NIF achieving first ignition in 2022 (3.15 MJ from 2.05 MJ) and a 2025 record of 8.6 MJ from 2.08 MJ of laser energy (target gain above 4), while JET's final D–T campaign in late 2023 produced 69 MJ (announced February 2024); the Lawson criterion nTτ_E ≳ 3×10²¹ keV s m⁻³ sets the target. Unknown: sustained net electricity, tritium breeding, materials and economics; ITER's revised baseline delays first plasma to 2034 and D–T to 2039, EAST held a high-confinement plasma for 1,066 s in January 2025 and WEST for 1,337 s in February 2025, and SPARC, Wendelstein 7-X and private tokamaks are the main programmes.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Lawson, J. D. (1957). Some criteria for a power producing thermonuclear reactor. Proceedings of the Physical Society. Section B, 70, 6–10.&#10;- Abu-Shawareb, H., Acree, R., Adams, P., et al. (2024). Achievement of target gain larger than unity in an inertial fusion experiment. Physical Review Letters, 132, 065102.&#10;- Wesson, J. (2011). Tokamaks (4th ed.). Oxford University Press.">
          <outline text="Lawson criterion" _note="Magnetic fusion needs nTτ_E above the D–T ignition threshold; inertial fusion uses areal density and ρR."/>
          <outline text="NIF ignition" _note="The 2022 shot produced more fusion energy than laser energy on target, and the April 2025 shot reached 8.6 MJ from 2.08 MJ, but wall-plug gain and repetition rate remain far off."/>
          <outline text="Tokamaks and ITER" _note="ITER aims at Q=10 but its revised baseline delays first plasma to 2034 and D–T to 2039; EAST (1,066 s, January 2025) and WEST (1,337 s, February 2025) set long-pulse records."/>
          <outline text="Private fusion" _note="Commonwealth Fusion's SPARC, TAE and others target net energy in the 2030s; no private plant has demonstrated net electricity."/>
        </outline>
        <outline text="Machine learning in physics" _note="boom since 2010s · John Hopfield, Geoffrey Hinton, Yann LeCun, Giuseppe Carleo, Kyle Cranmer · open question, as of 2026-11-08&#10;Known: machine learning is standard in collider event selection, gravitational-wave denoising, survey astronomy, materials screening and neural quantum states, and physics-informed networks solve PDEs; the variational principle E[ψ]=⟨ψ|H|ψ⟩/⟨ψ|ψ⟩ ≥ E0 underlies neural wavefunctions, and the 2024 Nobel Prize in Physics went to John Hopfield and Geoffrey Hinton for foundational work on artificial neural networks. Unknown: whether ML can discover new fundamental laws or deliver calibrated uncertainty and interpretability; no AI-discovered fundamental law exists as of 2026, with Carleo–Troyer neural quantum states (2017) a landmark physics-specific result.&#10;Sources:&#10;- Hopfield, J. J. (1982). Neural networks and physical systems with emergent collective computational abilities. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences, 79, 2554–2558.&#10;- Carleo, G., &amp; Troyer, M. (2017). Solving the quantum many-body problem with artificial neural networks. Science, 355, 602–606.&#10;- Carleo, G., Cirac, I., Cranmer, K., et al. (2019). Machine learning and the physical sciences. Reviews of Modern Physics, 91, 045002.">
          <outline text="Neural quantum states" _note="Carleo and Troyer (2017) represented many-body wavefunctions with restricted Boltzmann machines, launching ML-based quantum simulation."/>
          <outline text="ML for colliders" _note="Deep networks tag jets and search for anomalies at the LHC, but no new particle has been found this way."/>
          <outline text="Physics-informed networks" _note="Raissi et al. (2019) embedded PDE residuals in loss functions, enabling data-driven solutions of physical systems."/>
          <outline text="AI in astronomy" _note="Survey classifiers and generative models handle billions of galaxies and transients; interpretability and selection biases remain open."/>
        </outline>
      </outline>
    </outline>
  </body>
</opml>
